Each frozen case runs through \fBevery input/output shape\fR — AoH, HoA and HoH on
the left crossed with the same on the right and with both output shapes, 18
joins per case — and its output column names are checked separately, because a
join whose answer has no rows is exactly what several of the reference cases are
about and a row\-by\-row comparison cannot see the names. Beyond the tables the
file pins row order against pandas\*(Aq \f(CW\*(C`sort=False\*(C'\fR, R\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`by\*(C'\fR/\f(CW\*(C`by.x\*(C'\fR/\f(CW\*(C`by.y\*(C'\fR and
pandas\*(Aq \f(CW\*(C`left_on\*(C'\fR/\f(CW\*(C`right_on\*(C'\fR spellings, every error path the references
document, and double\-valued keys with dyadic literals.
.PP
The two tables are generated by \f(CW\*(C`t/merge.R.pandas.R\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`t/merge.R.pandas.py\*(C'\fR,
committed beside the test; the test itself never runs R or python, and needs
neither installed. Both blocks reproduce byte\-identically from the generators,
and the pandas block is byte\-identical under pandas 2.2.3 and 3.0.4, so nothing
frozen there is specific to a release.
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`t/merge.t\*(C'\fR also gained the cases a data frame cannot express, and so no
reference case can reach: an AoH row missing the key column entirely, a missing
non\-key cell, the \f(CW0\fR / \f(CW\*(Aq0\*(Aq\fR / \f(CW\*(Aq0.0\*(Aq\fR key identity, a NUL byte inside a key, a
wide character, \f(CW\*(C`Inf\*(C'\fR/\f(CW\*(C`\-Inf\*(C'\fR/\f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR keys, and blessed frames.
.PP
\fIAn \fR\f(CI\*(C`undef\*(C'\fR\fI join key matches nothing — which is not "the pandas \fR\f(CI\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR\fI rule"\fR
.IX Subsection "An undef join key matches nothing — which is not ""the pandas NaN rule"""
.PP
The documentation for \f(CW\*(C`merge\*(C'\fR credited its treatment of a missing join key to
pandas, and \f(CW\*(C`LikeR.xs\*(C'\fR credited it to R\*(Aqs default. Both attributions were wrong,
in the same direction: \fBboth references match a missing key to a missing key.\fR
On \f(CW\*(C`merge.Rd\*(C'\fR\*(Aqs own \f(CW\*(C`incomparables\*(C'\fR example the two agree with each other
exactly and disagree with \f(CW\*(C`merge\*(C'\fR:
.PP
The behaviour is right and unchanged — it is SQL\*(Aqs rule for a \f(CW\*(C`NULL\*(C'\fR key, which
is \f(CW\*(C`merge(..., incomparables = NA)\*(C'\fR in R, the line \f(CW\*(C`merge.Rd\*(C'\fR itself runs, and
what \f(CW\*(C`merge\*(C'\fR\*(Aqs documentation describes everywhere else. Only the credit was
wrong; R\*(Aqs default is \f(CW\*(C`incomparables = NULL\*(C'\fR. The corrected wording says which
rule it is and that it is not either reference\*(Aqs default, and
\&\f(CW\*(C`t/merge.R.pandas.t\*(C'\fR now asserts the divergence — with R\*(Aqs own
\&\f(CW\*(C`incomparables = NA\*(C'\fR answers frozen beside the generalised ones — so changing it
later has to be deliberate.
.PP
\fI11 leak checks reported Devel::Cover\*(Aqs own counters as leaks\fR
.IX Subsection "11 leak checks reported Devel::Cover's own counters as leaks"
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`t/vif_hoslem.t\*(C'\fR failed under \f(CW\*(C`cover\*(C'\fR with 37 leaks attributed to a line of
\&\f(CW\*(C`hosmer_lemeshow\*(C'\fR that allocates nothing (\f(CW\*(C`next unless defined ... &&
looks\e_like\e_number ...\*(C'\fR), and the leaked SVs were bare \f(CW\*(C`IV\*(C'\fRs holding values like
\&\f(CW98113961175368\fR — pointers. They are Devel::Cover\*(Aqs per\-line counters, which
are allocated inside whichever block happens to be running and which
\&\f(CW\*(C`Test::LeakTrace\*(C'\fR then counts; the same test reports 0 leaks on a plain perl.
.PP
Around ninety test files already guard their leak checks with
\&\f(CW$INC{\*(AqDevel/Cover.pm\*(Aq}\fR for this reason. Eleven did not:
\&\f(CW\*(C`t/age_standardize.t\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`t/dunn_test.t\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`t/effect_sizes.t\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`t/friedman_test.t\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CW\*(C`t/glm_families.t\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`t/ks_test.R.scipy.t\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`t/mcnemar_test.t\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`t/prop_test.t\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CW\*(C`t/tied.frames.t\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`t/vif_hoslem.t\*(C'\fR and — for an import it never used —
\&\f(CW\*(C`t/_parse_csv.t\*(C'\fR. Four of them failed; the rest passed only by luck, since
whether the counters land inside the measured block depends on which lines get
their first coverage there, which moves with file order. All of them are guarded
now, in the two forms the suite already uses, and the whole suite passes under
\&\f(CW\*(C`Devel::Cover\*(C'\fR (143 files, 35008 tests) as well as without it (143 files, 35577
tests — the difference is the leak checks, which still run and still report 0
leaks outside coverage mode).
.PP
\fR\f(CI\*(C`pt\*(C'\fR\fI near zero and \fR\f(CI\*(C`pf\*(C'\fR\fI\*(Aqs upper tail lost up to eight digits to a cancellation\fR
.IX Subsection "pt near zero and pf's upper tail lost up to eight digits to a cancellation"
.PP
\&\f(CWincbeta()\fR, the regularized incomplete beta every t, F and binomial tail is
built on, took only \f(CW\*(C`x\*(C'\fR and re\-formed \f(CW\*(C`1 \- x\*(C'\fR by subtraction. Its reflected
branch — \f(CW\*(C`I_x(a,b) = 1 \- I_{1\-x}(b,a)\*(C'\fR, taken exactly when \f(CW\*(C`x\*(C'\fR is the side near
\&\f(CW1\fR — then needs that complement, and forming it as \f(CW\*(C`1.0 \- x\*(C'\fR is catastrophic
cancellation there. Once \f(CW\*(C`|1 \- x|\*(C'\fR fell below \f(CW\*(C`2^\-53\*(C'\fR it collapsed to \f(CW0\fR and
took the whole tail with it:
.PP
Every caller had the complement exactly, and was throwing it away: \f(CW\*(C`pf\*(C'\fR builds
\&\f(CW\*(C`x = df1·f/D\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`1\-x = df2/D\*(C'\fR over one denominator, \f(CW\*(C`pt\*(C'\fR has
\&\f(CW\*(C`x = df/(df + t²)\*(C'\fR against \f(CW\*(C`t²/(df + t²)\*(C'\fR, and \f(CW\*(C`pbinom\*(C'\fR\*(Aqs lower tail is
\&\f(CW\*(C`I_{1\-p}(n\-k, k+1)\*(C'\fR with \f(CW\*(C`p\*(C'\fR itself to hand. So the fix is to pass both —
\&\f(CW\*(C`incbeta_xy(a, b, x, y)\*(C'\fR, which is R\*(Aqs own split (\f(CWbratio()\fR takes \f(CW\*(C`x\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`y\*(C'\fR
as separate arguments for this reason), with \f(CWincbeta()\fR kept as the
one\-argument wrapper for the callers that genuinely have only \f(CW\*(C`x\*(C'\fR, such as a
bisection midpoint.
.PP
Worst error against \f(CW\*(C`mpmath\*(C'\fR at \f(CW\*(C`mp.dps = 80\*(C'\fR, over a 372\-point grid:
.PP
The two tails also add up again: \f(CW\*(C`pf(1e\-12, 1, 1e6)\*(C'\fR\*(Aqs lower and upper summed
to \f(CW\*(C`1 + 7.98e\-07\*(C'\fR before, which is what first showed the bug.
.PP
This reached \f(CW\*(C`t_test\*(C'\fR, whose p\-value is \f(CW\*(C`1 \-\*(C'\fR that tail: a statistic small
enough on enough degrees of freedom had its p\-value pinned at exactly \f(CW1\fR
instead of \f(CW\*(C`1 \- 4e\-7\*(C'\fR. \f(CW\*(C`d_pf\*(C'\fR now calls \f(CWpf()\fR rather than repeating its
expression, so the two can no longer drift apart on the complement argument.
.PP
The one place \f(CW\*(C`incbeta_xy\*(C'\fR does \fBnot\fR help is the Clopper\-Pearson upper bound
for a handful of successes in ~1e9 trials, which still carries ~2e\-9 of relative
error. The cancellation there is in the continued fraction\*(Aqs own argument during
bisection, not in a complement a caller could have supplied, so \f(CW\*(C`t/binom_test.R.scipy.t\*(C'\fR\*(Aqs
existing note — that fixing it properly means porting \f(CWbratio()\fR — still stands.
.PP
\fR\f(CI\*(C`t_test\*(C'\fR\fI rejected \fR\f(CI\*(C`conf.level\*(C'\fR\fI, the spelling its own documentation lists\fR
.IX Subsection "t_test rejected conf.level, the spelling its own documentation lists"
.PP
.Vb 2
\& t_test(\e@x, \e@y, \*(Aqconf.level\*(Aq => 0.99);
\& # t_test: unknown argument \*(Aqconf.level\*(Aq
.Ve
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`t_test\*(C'\fR accepted only the underscored \f(CW\*(C`conf_level\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`var_equal\*(C'\fR, while its
parameter table in this file has always documented the argument as
\&\f(CW\*(C`conf.level\*(C'\fR, and while every sibling in the module — \f(CW\*(C`var_test\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CW\*(C`wilcox_test\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`prop_test\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`cmh_test\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`glm\*(C'\fR and the rest — already took both
spellings. \f(CW\*(C`var.equal\*(C'\fR, which is what R calls it, was refused too. Both dotted
forms now work, and the parameter table records the aliases.
.PP
\fR\f(CI\*(C`rank\*(C'\fR\fI, \fR\f(CI\*(C`wilcox_test\*(C'\fR\fI and \fR\f(CI\*(C`ks_test\*(C'\fR\fI are 1.6× to 2.3× faster\fR
.IX Subsection "rank, wilcox_test and ks_test are 1.6× to 2.3× faster"
.PP
These were still sorting through \f(CWqsort()\fR, whose comparator the compiler cannot
inline; the module\*(Aqs own \f(CWLIKER_DEFINE_SORT()\fR introsort and \f(CWnv_sort()\fR were
already used by the three internal rankers but not by these. Ordering \fIn\fR
records costs O(\fIn\fR log \fIn\fR) comparisons and an indirect call on each is most of
what such a sort costs — the figure \f(CW\*(C`LIKER_DEFINE_SORT\*(C'\fR\*(Aqs own comment records is
210µs against \f(CWqsort()\fR\*(Aqs 355µs on 5000 NVs.
.PP
Measured on 20,000 doubles:
.PP
\&\f(CWrank_and_count_ties()\fR, which \f(CW\*(C`wilcox_test\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`ks_test\*(C'\fR and five other functions
share, had been sorting \f(CW\*(C`RankInfo\*(C'\fR records through \f(CW\*(C`cmp_nv3\*(C'\fR — a comparator that
reads a bare \f(CW\*(C`NV\*(C'\fR. That worked only because \f(CW\*(C`val\*(C'\fR is the struct\*(Aqs first member
and a pointer to a struct is a pointer to its first member: true, but fragile as
well as slow. It has a generated ordering of its own now.
.PP
What is left in \f(CW\*(C`rank\*(C'\fR is no longer the sort. Reading the same 20,000 values back
out of an \f(CW\*(C`AV\*(C'\fR in plain perl costs 0.37 ms, so the gather loop and the \f(CW\*(C`newSVnv\*(C'\fR
per result are now most of the call.
.PP
\fIFive new cross\-validation files, from R\*(Aqs and SciPy\*(Aqs own test suites\fR
.IX Subsection "Five new cross-validation files, from R's and SciPy's own test suites"
.PP
4,444 tests, taking their cases from the references\*(Aq suites and documented
examples rather than inventing them, in the manner of the existing
\&\f(CW\*(C`t/*.R.scipy.t\*(C'\fR files. Expected values are frozen literals; the generators are
committed beside each test and are never run by it, so nothing here needs R,
python or \f(CW\*(C`mpmath\*(C'\fR at install time.
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`t_test\*(C'\fR had no cross\-validation at all before this, which is how the
\&\f(CW\*(C`conf.level\*(C'\fR croak above survived. Each documented case is crossed over the
whole argument space its function exposes — alternative × \f(CW\*(C`var_equal\*(C'\fR × \f(CW\*(C`mu\*(C'\fR ×
\&\f(CW\*(C`conf.level\*(C'\fR × \f(CW\*(C`paired\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`correct\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`exact\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`p\*(C'\fR — because a reference case
exercised only at its defaults pins one code path out of dozens.
.PP
Two things fell out of writing them.
.PP
\&\fR\f(CB\*(C`t/tukey.t\*(C'\fR\fB\*(Aqs tolerances understate the \fR\f(CB\*(C`ptukey\*(C'\fR\fB port by ten orders of
magnitude.\fR It checks \f(CW\*(C`qtukey\*(C'\fR to an absolute \f(CW1e\-3\fR and the \f(CW\*(C`TukeyHSD\*(C'\fR columns
to \f(CW1e\-4\fR, where the Copenhaver & Holland port actually agrees with R to
\&\f(CW3.0e\-14\fR and \f(CW2.5e\-12\fR over the new grid. A \f(CW1e\-3\fR limit would not notice the
port being replaced by a normal approximation, which is the regression it exists
to catch. The new file checks it properly; the old one is left alone.
.PP
\&\fBA tail probability needs an absolute tolerance, not a relative one, across NV
widths.\fR \f(CW\*(C`ptukey\*(C'\fR\*(Aqs internals are deliberately plain \f(CW\*(C`double\*(C'\fR, exactly as R\*(Aqs
\&\f(CW\*(C`src/nmath/ptukey.c\*(C'\fR has them, so the quadrature does not move with perl\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`NV\*(C'\fR
— but its \fIargument\fR \f(CW\*(C`q = |diff| / se\*(C'\fR comes from the \f(CW\*(C`aov\*(C'\fR mean square, which
is computed in \f(CW\*(C`NV\*(C'\fR. On the chickwts \f(CW\*(C`horsebean\-casein\*(C'\fR comparison, where
\&\f(CW\*(C`p adj\*(C'\fR is \f(CW3.07e\-08\fR:
.PP
Every width agrees to about \f(CW1e\-14\fR absolute, which is all a probability in the
\&\f(CW1e\-8\fR tail can be asked for. Conversely \f(CW\*(C`pf\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`pt\*(C'\fR come out three to four
orders \fImore\fR accurate on the wider widths (\f(CW2.74e\-11\fR → \f(CW1.1e\-15\fR for \f(CW\*(C`pf\*(C'\fR\*(Aqs
upper tail), which is the evidence that what is left there is the continued
fraction\*(Aqs convergence and not another cancellation — a cancellation does not
improve with the working precision.
.PP
\fR\f(CI\*(C`qf\*(C'\fR\fI is more accurate than R\*(Aqs own \fR\f(CI\*(C`qf\*(C'\fR\fI in the far lower tail\fR
.IX Subsection "qf is more accurate than R's own qf in the far lower tail"
.PP
Writing the file above turned up a disagreement in the other direction, so it is
recorded rather than quietly reconciled. Asked for the F quantile deep in the
lower tail, R bottoms out at a fixed resolution:
.PP
R is out by 122% there. Over the 108\-point grid in \f(CW\*(C`t/pf_pt_tails.R.mpmath.t\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CW\*(C`qf\*(C'\fR is within \f(CW7.6e\-15\fR relative of 80\-digit truth at every point and R is out
by as much as \f(CW1.22\fR, on 17 of them. The test asserts \f(CW\*(C`qf\*(C'\fR against \f(CW\*(C`mpmath\*(C'\fR and
separately asserts that R really is the worse of the two, so that "fixing" \f(CW\*(C`qf\*(C'\fR
towards R would fail loudly instead of passing quietly.
.PP
Also recorded there: \f(CW\*(C`qtukey\*(C'\fR is \fInot\fR an exact inverse of \f(CW\*(C`ptukey\*(C'\fR, in R or
here. R\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`qtukey.c\*(C'\fR is a secant iteration that stops once successive iterates
differ by less than a hardcoded \f(CW\*(C`const static double eps = 0.0001\*(C'\fR — an absolute
\&\f(CW1e\-4\fR in \f(CW\*(C`q\*(C'\fR, not a relative tolerance on \f(CW\*(C`p\*(C'\fR — so \f(CW\*(C`ptukey(qtukey(p))\*(C'\fR recovers
\&\f(CW\*(C`p\*(C'\fR only to about \f(CW1e\-7\fR. R\*(Aqs own worst round trip over that grid is
\&\f(CW1.2744607e\-07\fR, and this port\*(Aqs is the same to the digits printed, which is the
strongest evidence in the file that the port really is faithful rather than
merely close.
.SS "0.312 2026\-08\-28 CDT"
.IX Subsection "0.312 2026-08-28 CDT"
\fI80\-bit x87 arithmetic broke four test files on 32\-bit x86\fR
.IX Subsection "80-bit x87 arithmetic broke four test files on 32-bit x86"
.PP
A CPAN smoker on \f(CW\*(C`i686\-linux\-64int\*(C'\fR (perl 5.32.1) failed 73 subtests across
\&\f(CW\*(C`t/bedroc.python.t\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`t/quantile.R.t\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`t/var_sd_cov.R.t\*(C'\fR and
\&\f(CW\*(C`t/wilcox_test.R.scipy.t\*(C'\fR. None of it reproduced here: the local matrix varies
how wide an NV is \fIstored\fR — double, long double, \f(CW\*(C`_\|_float128\*(C'\fR — but every perl
in it is x86\-64, so all five agree on doing the arithmetic in SSE at exactly
that width.
.PP
32\-bit x86 does not. It computes doubles in the x87 register file at 80 bits and
rounds only when a value is spilled to memory, so \f(CW\*(C`FLT_EVAL_METHOD\*(C'\fR is 2 where
an SSE build reports 0. C99 says a cast or an assignment rounds to the declared
type, but gcc implements that only under \f(CW\*(C`\-fexcess\-precision=standard\*(C'\fR, and
\&\f(CW\*(C`\-std=gnu99\*(C'\fR — which the C99 probe in \f(CW\*(C`Makefile.PL\*(C'\fR tries first — selects
\&\f(CW\*(C`\-fexcess\-precision=fast\*(C'\fR, which keeps the extra bits. A product therefore
carried more precision than a double has, and every place that split one into an
integer part and a remainder picked a different integer:
.PP
One cause, four faces. \f(CW\*(C`bedroc\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`auroc\*(C'\fR size a bucket with
\&\f(CW\*(C`ceil(frac * N)\*(C'\fR, so every count came back one too high — each wrong enrichment
factor in the smoker\*(Aqs report has an eleven under it where a ten belongs, and
\&\f(CW\*(C`n.active\*(C'\fR was 4 where 3 was wanted, 21 where 20 was. \f(CW\*(C`quantile\*(C'\fR forms
\&\f(CW\*(C`h = (n \- 1) * p\*(C'\fR and interpolates whenever \f(CW\*(C`h\*(C'\fR is not an integer, so at
\&\fIn\fR = 1001 and \fIp\fR = 7/1000 it interpolated between two order statistics where R
returns one of them exactly. \f(CW\*(C`var\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`sd\*(C'\fR disagreed with themselves in the
last digit between the tied\-array and plain\-array paths. And \f(CW\*(C`wilcox_test\*(C'\fR found
its pseudomedian by a root search over a step function, where a perturbed
iterate does not move the answer by an ulp but lands on a different flat step:
.PP
The fix is in two layers. \f(CW\*(C`Makefile.PL\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`dist.ini\*(C'\fR now trial\-compile
\&\f(CW\*(C`\-fexcess\-precision=standard\*(C'\fR and add it when the compiler takes it, alongside
the existing per\-vendor C99 probe; gcc and clang both exit non\-zero on an \f(CW\*(C`\-f\*(C'\fR
switch they do not implement, and MSVC is skipped as before, needing nothing —
it has defaulted to SSE2 on x86 since VS2012, and \f(CW\*(C`/fp:precise\*(C'\fR rounds to the
declared type. That flag on its own fixes all 73. It is still not the whole
answer, because it is not everywhere: an older clang, a vendor cc, or any
compiler whose only C99 mode is a wide\-register one will not take it, and this
module is expected to build on all of them.
.PP
So \f(CW\*(C`LikeR.xs\*(C'\fR also gains \f(CWnv_narrow()\fR, which forces the store through a
\&\f(CW\*(C`volatile NV\*(C'\fR by hand at the seven places where a product is split into an
integer part and a remainder: both passes of \f(CW\*(C`quantile\*(C'\fR — which must agree, or
the partial sort places the wrong order statistics — plus \f(CW\*(C`dens_quantile7\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CW\*(C`_qcut_core\*(C'\fR, and the bucket counts in \f(CW\*(C`bedroc\*(C'\fR twice and \f(CW\*(C`auroc\*(C'\fR once. Those
are the sites where the extra bits change \fIan answer\fR rather than its last
digit, and by itself it fixes 63 of the 73. The other 10 are last\-digit work —
\&\f(CW\*(C`var\*(C'\fR agreeing with itself between the tied\-array and plain\-array paths, and the
root search landing on the same step — which is what the flag is for and what no
reasonable amount of hand\-placed rounding would buy.
.PP
Rounding to the NV\*(Aqs own width is the right answer at every width, not a
concession to double: 7/1000 times 1000 rounds to exactly 7 in a double and in a
long double alike. So both layers are inert on the long\-double and quadmath
builds, which is what the matrix confirms.
.PP
No new tests were written, because the four cross\-validation files that caught
this are already the regression test — they are pinned to R and SciPy and they
fail without the fix. What was missing was a build that could run them the way
the smoker did, so \f(CW\*(C`./test.all.perls.pl\*(C'\fR gains a first\-class \f(CW\*(C`<perl>+x87\*(C'\fR row
that rebuilds the newest plain perl with \f(CW\*(C`\-mfpmath=387\*(C'\fR. It reproduced 63 of the
73 subtests by test number. One row is enough and it is skipped where there is
nothing to catch — an x87 register is exactly as wide as a long\-double NV, and
\&\f(CW\*(C`_\|_float128\*(C'\fR never touches the x87 stack — and \f(CW\*(C`\-\-no\-x87\*(C'\fR turns it off.
.PP
That row cannot see the other half of 32\-bit x86, and it is worth naming so the
gap is not mistaken for coverage: i386 returns a double in \f(CWst(0)\fR, so a callee
hands its caller all 80 bits, where the x86\-64 ABI returns in \f(CW\*(C`xmm0\*(C'\fR and narrows
for free. That is the half that moved \f(CW\*(C`wilcox_test\*(C'\fR, which is why it was the one
failure that could not be reproduced here at all; the assembly shows plain
\&\f(CW\*(C`\-std=gnu99\*(C'\fR emitting \f(CW\*(C`fdivl\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`ret\*(C'\fR with no rounding between them, and the
flag inserting the \f(CW\*(C`fstpl\*(C'\fR/\f(CW\*(C`fldl\*(C'\fR round\-trip that narrows the result. Only a
real 32\-bit perl will exercise it.
.PP
\fR\f(CIcor_test()\fR\fI: five disagreements with R, one of which moves p\-values\fR
.IX Subsection "cor_test(): five disagreements with R, one of which moves p-values"
.PP
The tie\-corrected variance is the one that matters. \f(CW\*(C`cor\e_test(method =>
\&\*(Aqkendall\*(Aq)\*(C'\fR divided the Kendall score by \f(CW\*(C`n(n\-1)(2n+5)/18\*(C'\fR, which is R\*(Aqs
\&\f(CW\*(C`var_S\*(C'\fR only when there are no ties — and with no ties and \fIn\fR < 50 the branch
is not reached at all, so the correction was missing in exactly the case that
needs it. R builds it from the tie\-group sizes of each vector:
.PP
.Vb 1
\& var_S = (v0 \- vt \- vu)/18 + v1/(2n(n\-1)) + v2/(9n(n\-1)(n\-2))
.Ve
.PP
On fifteen points of tied integer data:
.PP
A factor of 1.57 on the p\-value, across 0.05. Integer scores, counts and Likert
data are all ties, which is most of what a Kendall correlation is asked for.
.PP
Four more, in the same function:
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBTies of odd group size were not ties.\fR The Spearman branch decided
\&\f(CW\*(C`TIES\*(C'\fR by looking for a fractional average rank, and a group of three
averages to a whole number: \f(CW\*(C`y = (1,1,1,2,2,2,3,3,3,4,4,4)\*(C'\fR ranks as
2, 2, 2, 5, 5, 5, 8, 8, 8, 11, 11, 11 and was called tie\-free, which sent it
to the exact branch R reserves for data that has none. It is now R\*(Aqs test —
a repeated \fIvalue\fR — taken from the tie walk \f(CWrank_data()\fR was already doing.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBSpearman\*(Aqs \fR\f(CB\*(C`statistic\*(C'\fR\fB under ties.\fR R always forms \fIS\fR as
\&\f(CW\*(C`(n³ − n)(1 − ρ)/6\*(C'\fR; the sum of squared rank differences is the same number
only without ties. 0.311 reported the sum: \f(CW793.5\fR where R gives
\&\f(CW865.39724699477085\fR. It is now the identity, on every path.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBThe Pearson confidence interval ignored \fR\f(CB\*(C`alternative\*(C'\fR\fB.\fR R switches on it —
\&\f(CW\*(C`less\*(C'\fR is \f(CW\*(C`(\-1, tanh(z + σ·qnorm(cl))]\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`greater\*(C'\fR is
\&\f(CW\*(C`[tanh(z \- σ·qnorm(cl)), 1)\*(C'\fR. The two\-sided interval came back whatever was
asked for: \f(CW\*(C`[0.5217431448512, 0.9680507713838]\*(C'\fR for \f(CWalternative =>
\&\*(Aqgreater\*(Aq\fR where R gives \f(CW\*(C`[0.6029901323843, 1]\*(C'\fR. The p\-value was never
wrong, only the interval, which is the kind of error that reads as an answer.
There is also no interval below \fIn\fR = 4 now, as in R.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBThe continuity correction at a score of zero.\fR R applies
\&\f(CW\*(C`S <\- sign(S) * (abs(S) \- 1)\*(C'\fR, and \f(CWsign(0)\fR is 0, so a score of exactly 0
stays 0. Subtracting a signum that treats 0 as negative moved it to +1:
thirty points whose score is 0 reported \f(CW\*(C`z = 0.019410388389502\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CW\*(C`p = 0.98451372323408\*(C'\fR where the answer is \f(CW\*(C`z = 0\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`p = 1\*(C'\fR.
.PP
One divergence went the other way and is now gone. The exact Kendall
distribution summed its lower tail from \f(CW\*(C`dp[max_inv]\*(C'\fR downwards, the end of the
array that has accumulated every rounding the recurrence made; the distribution
of inversions is symmetric, so both tails are now summed from \f(CW\*(C`dp[0]\*(C'\fR. Perfect
discordance at \fIn\fR = 10 returned \f(CW2.7557319223626736e\-07\fR and is now exactly
1/10!, \f(CW2.7557319223985891e\-07\fR, which is R\*(Aqs value. In the mirror case R is
the one that is 9.3e\-11 out and \f(CW\*(C`cor_test\*(C'\fR gives the exact double;
\&\f(CW\*(C`t/cor_test.kendall.R.t\*(C'\fR records that and asserts the combinatorics.
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`t/cor_test.kendall.R.t\*(C'\fR (1236 assertions) and \f(CW\*(C`t/cor_test.pearson.ci.R.t\*(C'\fR
(274) are new and generated from R; \f(CW\*(C`t/cor_test.spearman.R.t\*(C'\fR gained the tied
rows it had been quoting as a recorded divergence.
.PP
\fIKendall\*(Aqs counts, in O(n log n)\fR
.IX Subsection "Kendall's counts, in O(n log n)"
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`cor_test(method => \*(Aqkendall\*(Aq)\*(C'\fR walked every (\fIi\fR, \fIj\fR) pair to count
concordances — the O(\fIn\fR²) loop \f(CWcor()\fR had already replaced with Knight\*(Aqs
algorithm in 0.31. Both now come from one routine, which returns the tie
moments R\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`var_S\*(C'\fR needs along with the pair counts, so the correctness fix
above and this are the same edit:
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`cov(..., \*(Aqkendall\*(Aq)\*(C'\fR is the same sum over the full \fIn\fR × \fIn\fR space, so it is
2(C − D) and comes from the same counts: 1.85 s to 0.0061 s at \fIn\fR = 32 000.
R\*(Aqs own \f(CW\*(C`cov(method = "kendall")\*(C'\fR is O(\fIn\fR²), so this is now faster than the
reference rather than merely equal to it.
.PP
\fR\f(CIhist()\fR\fI: R\*(Aqs \fR\f(CIpretty()\fR\fI breakpoints\fR
.IX Subsection "hist(): R's pretty() breakpoints"
.PP
R does not cut the range into \f(CW\*(C`breaks\*(C'\fR equal pieces. It treats that number as a
suggestion and asks \f(CWpretty()\fR for round numbers, so the axis ends outside the
data and the interval count need not be the one requested:
.PP
.Vb 3
\& hist([1,2,2,3,4,7,9,10,11,15])
\&   breaks  0  2  4  6  8  10  12  14  16       was  1  5.75  10.5  15.25  20
\&   mids    1  3  5  7  9  11  13  15           was  3.375  8.125  12.875  17.625
.Ve
.PP
\&\f(CWR_pretty()\fR is ported from \f(CW\*(C`src/appl/pretty.c\*(C'\fR with \f(CW\*(C`pretty.default()\*(C'\fR\*(Aqs
parameters and \f(CW\*(C`hist.default()\*(C'\fR\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`min.n = 1\*(C'\fR; the counts come from a binary
search over the breakpoints carrying R\*(Aqs 1e\-7 fuzz, which is what puts a value
sitting a rounding error above a break into the bin below it. The default bin
count is R\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`nclass.Sturges\*(C'\fR, ⌈log₂ \fIn\fR + 1⌉ — the ceiling is part of it, and
a truncating cast had been turning \f(CW\*(C`log₂(13) + 1 = 4.70\*(C'\fR into 4.
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`t/hist.R.t\*(C'\fR pins all four returned vectors across thirteen datasets — a single
point, a constant column, data spanning zero, data of order 1e\-3 and 1e6 —
crossed with eight \f(CW\*(C`breaks\*(C'\fR settings. Every value matches R exactly, to the
last bit.
.PP
\fIArguments that are not numbers\fR
.IX Subsection "Arguments that are not numbers"
.PP
\&\f(CWSvNV()\fR turns a string that is not a number into 0 without a word, because the
\&\f(CW\*(C`numeric\*(C'\fR warning it would raise belongs to the caller\*(Aqs scope and an XSUB does
not run in one. So \f(CW\*(C`min(1, 2, \*(Aqabc\*(Aq)\*(C'\fR returned \fB0\fR — a value that is not
among its arguments and is not the smallest of them — and \f(CW\*(C`mean(1, 2, \*(Aqabc\*(Aq)\*(C'\fR
returned 1. \f(CW\*(C`undef\*(C'\fR had always been refused; this was the hole beside it.
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`mean\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`sum\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`min\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`max\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`median\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`var\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`sd\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`skew\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`kurtosis\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CW\*(C`quantile\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`scale\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`hist\*(C'\fR now croak, naming the argument or the cell. An
object that overloads its numeric conversion still counts as a number; a
reference that overloads nothing is now refused too, where \f(CWSvNV()\fR used to
fold in its address. The cross\-validation file that had been asserting the old
behaviour — \f(CW\*(C`t/read_table.na_strings.t\*(C'\fR, on an unmapped literal \f(CW\*(C`NA\*(C'\fR — asserts
the croak instead, which is a better signal than the warning it could not see.
.PP
\fR\f(CI\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR\fI in \fR\f(CImedian()\fR\fI and \fR\f(CIquantile()\fR\fI\fR
.IX Subsection "NaN in median() and quantile()"
.PP
No comparison sort can place a \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR: every comparison against one is false. So
which value ended up in the middle depended on where in the array it sat.
\&\f(CWmedian()\fR of 1..50 with one \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR in it returned 25.5, \f(CW\*(C`median([5, 1, NaN])\*(C'\fR
returned 5, and \f(CW\*(C`median([1, 2, NaN, 4])\*(C'\fR returned \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR. \f(CWquantile()\fR of that
same 50 answered \f(CW13.25\fR for the 25% and \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR for the 75%.
.PP
Both now propagate — one \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR in, \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR out — which is what \f(CW\*(C`sum\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`mean\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CW\*(C`var\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`sd\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`min\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`max\*(C'\fR already did here, and what keeps the 50% quantile
equal to \f(CWmedian()\fR. \f(CW\*(C`undef\*(C'\fR is still dropped by \f(CWquantile()\fR, as documented.
.PP
The five bandwidth selectors disagreed with each other about the same input:
\&\f(CW\*(C`bw_ucv\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`bw_bcv\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`bw_sj\*(C'\fR rejected a non\-finite value a layer down, while
\&\f(CW\*(C`bw_nrd0\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`bw_nrd\*(C'\fR returned a number — 7.5250570111922 for 1..50 with two
\&\f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fRs, computed from a variance and an interquartile range that are both
\&\f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR via a \f(CWmin()\fR that a \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR silently loses. All five now refuse it in the
shared reader, which is also what keeps a \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR out of the \f(CWqsort()\fR there:
\&\f(CWcmp_nv3()\fR cannot order one, and an inconsistent comparator makes \f(CWqsort()\fR
undefined behaviour rather than merely inaccurate.
.PP
\fIRagged frames\fR
.IX Subsection "Ragged frames"
.PP
A HoA frame whose columns have different lengths was fitted on whichever column
\&\f(CWhv_iternext()\fR reached first, so how many rows \f(CWlm()\fR, \f(CWglm()\fR and \f(CWprcomp()\fR
used moved with hash order from run to run: \f(CW\*(C`{ y => [1..6], x => [1,2,3] }\*(C'\fR
returned a perfect fit on three rows with \f(CW\*(C`df.residual = 1\*(C'\fR and said nothing.
All three now croak, naming the column and both lengths, as \f(CWcsort()\fR already
did; \f(CWprcomp()\fR checks a ragged AoA too.
.PP
This is stricter than R on purpose. \f(CW\*(C`data.frame()\*(C'\fR recycles a short column when
its length divides the longest — \f(CW\*(C`data.frame(y = 1:6, x = 1:3)\*(C'\fR quietly fits on
\&\f(CW\*(C`x\*(C'\fR repeated twice, and only \f(CW\*(C`1:4\*(C'\fR is an error there. Inventing observations is
not something to do quietly in a model fit.
.PP
\fR\f(CIscale()\fR\fI: named arguments\fR
.IX Subsection "scale(): named arguments"
.PP
Every other function in this module takes its options as trailing name/value
pairs. \f(CWscale()\fR read only a trailing hash reference, so a flat pair fell
through into the data, where the option name numified to 0 and was scaled along
with everything else: \f(CW\*(C`scale([1,2,3], center => 0)\*(C'\fR returned \fBfive\fR numbers
for a three\-element input. Both forms are now accepted and agree; an
unrecognised name is an error rather than a data point.
.PP
\fR\f(CIsurvfit()\fR\fI: where the curve reaches zero\fR
.IX Subsection "survfit(): where the curve reaches zero"
.PP
Greenwood\*(Aqs next variance term is \f(CW\*(C`d / (n.risk × (n.risk − d))\*(C'\fR, and the last
event takes every remaining subject, so \f(CW\*(C`n.risk == d\*(C'\fR and the variance of
\&\fIS\fR(\fIt\fR) is not defined from there on. R reports \f(CW\*(C`std.err\*(C'\fR as \f(CW\*(C`Inf\*(C'\fR and both
confidence limits as \f(CW\*(C`NA\*(C'\fR. All three came back 0, which is a number where there
is no answer, and a plausible\-looking one. They are \f(CW\*(C`undef\*(C'\fR now.
.PP
\fITwo smoker failures, both in the tests rather than the module\fR
.IX Subsection "Two smoker failures, both in the tests rather than the module"
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`LikeR.xs\*(C'\fR is unchanged here. Two failures came back from CPAN smokers on 0.31,
and in each case the assertion had been pinned to something that is a property of
the perl running it rather than of \f(CW\*(C`Stats::LikeR\*(C'\fR — and this machine\*(Aqs build
happens to satisfy it, which is why neither showed up before the reports arrived.
.PP
\f(CW\*(C`t/avals.t\*(C'\fR was counting \f(CW\*(C`FETCH\*(C'\fRes on a tied cell
.IX Subsection "t/avals.t was counting FETCHes on a tied cell"
.PP
The tied\-cell block built its frame by copying a tied scalar into an anonymous
hash, and used a \f(CW\*(C`FETCH\*(C'\fR that counted up so that a second fetch would be
visible:
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`perl
sub FETCH { my $s = shift; return $$s++ }   # a different value each FETCH
my $tied; tie $tied, \*(Aqt::AlwaysSeven\*(Aq;
my @got = avals([ { v => $tied } ], \*(Aqv\*(Aq);
is($got[0], 7, \*(Aqa tied cell is fetched once, at extraction time\*(Aq);\*(C'\fR
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`{ v => $tied }\*(C'\fR fetches the cell while the frame is being built, so what
\&\f(CWavals()\fR copied was already a plain number: the test never exercised the thing
its own name described, and what it actually measured was how many times that
perl runs get\-magic on one \f(CWsv_setsv()\fR. That count is not fixed. Measured
directly, \f(CW\*(C`perl\-5.10.1\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`perl\-5.12.5\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`perl\-5.44.0\*(C'\fR each run it once and
the caller sees 7; a smoker on 5.18.4 (\f(CW\*(C`x86_64\-linux\-thread\-multi\*(C'\fR) reported 8,
i.e. two runs on the one copy, and a second report of the same two subtests
followed.
.PP
The cell is now tied in place, so nothing touches it before \f(CWavals()\fR does, and
\&\f(CW\*(C`FETCH\*(C'\fR returns a constant, so nothing depends on how many times it is called:
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`perl
my %row;
tie $row{v}, \*(Aqt::TiedCell\*(Aq, 7;
\&\*(C'\fR
.PP
What the block asserts is now what it always claimed: building the frame fetches
nothing, extraction fetches at least once, the fetched value rather than the
tied SV comes back, the copy carries no tie magic, re\-reading it does not fetch
again, and it is exactly what \f(CWvals()\fR returns for the same frame. The HoH
branch and the HoA branch — which fills \f(CW\*(C`AvARRAY\*(C'\fR directly and so has its own
\&\f(CWnewSVsv()\fR — get the same treatment, 44 subtests to 55.
.PP
\f(CW\*(C`t/01.t\*(C'\fR compared a number of order 50 against an absolute \f(CW1e\-14\fR
.IX Subsection "t/01.t compared a number of order 50 against an absolute 1e-14"
.PP
The other report was the \f(CWlm()\fR F statistic, exactly 54 by algebra:
.PP
\&\`\`\`
.SH "got: 54"
.IX Header "got: 54"
.SH "expected: 54; diff = 1.4210854715202e\-14"
.IX Header "expected: 54; diff = 1.4210854715202e-14"
\&\`\`\`
.PP
\&\f(CWis_approx()\fR treated its \f(CW$epsilon\fR as an absolute bound, and 54 sits where the
spacing of a double is \f(CW\*(C`ulp(54) = 2**\-47 = 7.105e\-15\*(C'\fR. A bound of \f(CW1e\-14\fR is
therefore 1.41 ulps — the call site was demanding bit\-exactness of a number of
order 50, and the smoker\*(Aqs build simply reassociated the sum and landed two ulps
out (\f(CW1.4210854715202e\-14\fR is exactly \f(CW\*(C`2**\-46\*(C'\fR). The same \f(CW1e\-14\fR written
against a p\-value of order \f(CW1e\-9\fR is some 10⁵ ulps, which is what the number was
chosen to mean.
.PP
The allowance is now scaled by the size of the expected value, floored at the
absolute bound so that comparisons against 0 — and against anything below 1 —
keep exactly the tolerance they had:
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`perl
my $scale = abs($expected) > 1 ? abs($expected) : 1;
my $tol   = $epsilon * $scale;\*(C'\fR
.PP
The F statistic gets 76 ulps of headroom in place of one. The failure \f(CWdiag()\fR
also prints the tolerance now, so the next report of this shape can be read
without opening the file.
.PP
Every \f(CW\*(C`is_approx\*(C'\fR\-style call in all 138 test files was then parsed and its
tolerance compared against \f(CWulp(expected)\fR. Nine sites sat under eight ulps, all
of them in \f(CW\*(C`t/01.t\*(C'\fR and all now covered by the scaling:
.PP
Call sites with a tolerance of \f(CW1e\-13\fR or tighter against a computed value exist
in \f(CW\*(C`t/prcomp.t\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`t/t_test.t\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`t/value_counts.t\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`t/wilcox_test.t\*(C'\fR; each was
checked by hand and compares a p\-value, a count, an integer rank sum, or an
\&\f(CW\*(C`sdev\*(C'\fR of about 2.8, so those bounds are thousands of ulps wide and are left
alone.
.PP
Both files pass on \f(CW\*(C`perl\-5.44.0\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`perl\-5.10.1\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`perl\-5.12.5\*(C'\fR — \f(CW\*(C`t/01.t\*(C'\fR
1399 of 1399, \f(CW\*(C`t/avals.t\*(C'\fR 55 of 55 — the two older perls built in private trees
so that the distribution root\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`Makefile\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`blib\*(C'\fR stay as they were.
.SS "0.311 2026\-08\-28"
.IX Subsection "0.311 2026-08-28"
\fIavals\fR
.IX Subsection "avals"
.PP
new function to make code cleaner.  \f(CW\*(C`avals\*(C'\fR is the same as \f(CW\*(C`vals\*(C'\fR but returns an array instead of an array reference, so instead of \f(CW\*(C`@{ vals($df, \*(Aqcolname\*(Aq) }\*(C'\fR it will just be \f(CW\*(C`avals($df, \*(Aqcolname\*(Aq)\*(C'\fR to prevent the need for bracketing
.PP
\fICorrectness and portability fixes\fR
.IX Subsection "Correctness and portability fixes"
.PP
Nine defects found by cross\-validating against R 4.6.1 and mpmath, by running
the suite under UBSan and AddressSanitizer, and by reading for the conventions
in \f(CW\*(C`CLAUDE.md\*(C'\fR. Each is pinned by a test that fails without the fix:
\&\f(CW\*(C`t/cor_test.spearman.R.t\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`t/pt_qt.tails.R.t\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`t/minmax.nan.R.t\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CW\*(C`t/var_test.ci.R.t\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`t/portability.bugs.t\*(C'\fR.
.PP
\f(CW\*(C`cor_test(method => \*(Aqspearman\*(Aq)\*(C'\fR could not return
.IX Subsection "cor_test(method => 'spearman') could not return"
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`exact => 1\*(C'\fR ran an unbounded permutation walk. R has never enumerated past
\&\fIn\fR = 9 — \f(CW\*(C`n_small\*(C'\fR in \f(CW\*(C`src/library/stats/src/prho.c\*(C'\fR — whatever \f(CW\*(C`exact\*(C'\fR says,
but here the caller\*(Aqs flag was taken literally, and \fIn\fR! is 6.2 × 10²³ by
\&\fIn\fR = 24:
.PP
The enumeration is now capped at \fIn\fR ≤ 9 and 10 ≤ \fIn\fR ≤ 1290 goes to the AS 89
Edgeworth series, which is what R\*(Aqs \f(CWprho()\fR does. Past 1290 — R\*(Aqs own bound,
where \f(CW\*(C`n^3 \- n\*(C'\fR overflows an \f(CW\*(C`int\*(C'\fR — it falls back to the asymptotic \fIt\fR, as
before.
.PP
Spearman p\-values were R\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`exact = FALSE\*(C'\fR for every \fIn\fR ≥ 10
.IX Subsection "Spearman p-values were R's exact = FALSE for every n ≥ 10"
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`exact\*(C'\fR defaulted to \f(CW\*(C`(n < 10)\*(C'\fR. R\*(Aqs default is \f(CW\*(C`TRUE\*(C'\fR, and R serves it from
\&\f(CWprho()\fR for every \fIn\fR up to 1290, so the old default silently took R\*(Aqs
approximation branch:
.PP
Across 45 randomly generated pairs the p\-value now agrees with R to \f(CW3.5e\-14\fR.
.PP
Spearman\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`statistic\*(C'\fR changed meaning at \fIn\fR = 10
.IX Subsection "Spearman's statistic changed meaning at n = 10"
.PP
It was \fIS\fR from the exact branch and the \fIt\fR statistic from the other. It is
now \fIS\fR on every path, as R reports it. This is a change to a documented
field — the note saying to compare \f(CW\*(C`estimate\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`p.value\*(C'\fR rather than
\&\f(CW\*(C`statistic\*(C'\fR described the old behaviour and is gone.
.PP
\f(CW\*(C`qt\*(C'\fR saturated, and \f(CW\*(C`pt\*(C'\fR collapsed to zero, in the far tail
.IX Subsection "qt saturated, and pt collapsed to zero, in the far tail"
.PP
\&\f(CWpt_upper()\fR computed the tail as \f(CW\*(C`I_z(df/2, 1/2)\*(C'\fR with \f(CW\*(C`z = df/(df + t²)\*(C'\fR and
nothing else. \f(CW\*(C`z\*(C'\fR underflows well before \f(CW\*(C`t²\*(C'\fR overflows, so the tail became a
flat \f(CW0\fR; and because \f(CWqt_tail()\fR bisects against it, and its bracket stopped
at \f(CWsqrt(DBL_MAX)\fR, the quantile converged onto the ceiling and returned it —
a plausible number, not an error:
.PP
\&\f(CWpt_upper()\fR now carries the Abramowitz & Stegun 26.5.4 branch from R\*(Aqs
\&\f(CW\*(C`nmath/pt.c\*(C'\fR, which evaluates the tail in logs once \f(CW\*(C`1 + (t/df)·t\*(C'\fR passes
\&\f(CW1e100\fR and so has nothing left to underflow, and the bracket runs to
\&\f(CW\*(C`NV_MAX/2\*(C'\fR and reports \f(CW\*(C`Inf\*(C'\fR when the quantile genuinely exceeds what an \f(CW\*(C`NV\*(C'\fR
can name. \f(CW\*(C`pt\*(C'\fR now matches R exactly from \f(CW\-1e150\fR to \f(CW\-1e300\fR.
.PP
\f(CW\*(C`min\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`max\*(C'\fR skipped a \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR unless it came first
.IX Subsection "min and max skipped a NaN unless it came first"
.PP
Every comparison against a \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR is false, so \f(CW\*(C`if (v < mn)\*(C'\fR passed over one and
kept a real number — but a \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR in the first position stayed. The answer
depended on where in the array it sat:
.PP
.Vb 2
\& min([NaN, 1, 2])   was NaN      min([1, 2, NaN])   was 1
\& min([1, NaN, 2])   was 1        max([1, 2, NaN])   was 2
.Ve
.PP
R gives \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR for all of them, and \f(CW\*(C`sum\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`mean\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`var\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`sd\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`median\*(C'\fR here
already did. Both now propagate \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR from any position, in the flat argument
list and the array\-reference forms alike.
.PP
\f(CW\*(C`var_test\*(C'\fR\*(Aqs confidence interval lost up to \f(CW2.5e\-11\fR
.IX Subsection "var_test's confidence interval lost up to 2.5e-11"
.PP
It divided by a private \f(CWqf_bisection()\fR that stopped on an \fBabsolute\fR
\&\f(CW\*(C`high \- low < 1e\-12\*(C'\fR. The F quantile it divides by is not of order 1 — at
\&\f(CW\*(C`conf.level = 1 \- 2^\-20\*(C'\fR on nine and nine degrees of freedom it is about
\&\f(CW0.016\fR — so an absolute stop is a relative error that grows as the quantile
shrinks:
.PP
It now uses the same \f(CWd_qf()\fR that the exported \f(CW\*(C`qf\*(C'\fR does, which has a relative
stop and agrees with mpmath at \f(CW\*(C`mp.dps = 60\*(C'\fR to about \f(CW1e\-16\fR. Over 864
generated cases the interval agrees with R to \f(CW2.1e\-14\fR. \f(CW\*(C`qf_bisection\*(C'\fR had no
other caller and is gone; carrying two F quantiles of different accuracy in one
file was the underlying defect.
.PP
\f(CW\*(C`var_test\*(C'\fR\*(Aqs p\-value was formed by subtraction
.IX Subsection "var_test's p-value was formed by subtraction"
.PP
It computed the upper tail as \f(CW\*(C`1 \- pf(F)\*(C'\fR, which is what
"F and z tail p\-values" says every F test here stopped
doing — \f(CW\*(C`var_test\*(C'\fR was listed neither among those converted nor among the three
known to remain subtractive. It now uses \f(CWpf_upper()\fR, which builds its own
complement as \f(CW\*(C`df2 / (df1·F + df2)\*(C'\fR without subtracting. R writes the
subtraction too, so in the far tail this is now \fBmore accurate than R\fR;
measured against mpmath over the same 864 cases:
.PP
R\*(Aqs worst is \f(CW1.0\fR because \f(CW\*(C`1 \- pf(F)\*(C'\fR reaches a flat \f(CW0\fR once the lower tail is
within an ulp of \f(CW1\fR. On \f(CW\*(C`c(3,1,4,1,5,9,2,6,5,3,5)\*(C'\fR against
\&\f(CW\*(C`c(1,2,4,8,16,32,64)/1024\*(C'\fR, two\-sided: mpmath \f(CW1.08732387158297135e\-11\fR,
\&\f(CW\*(C`Stats::LikeR\*(C'\fR \f(CW1.0873238715829779e\-11\fR, R \f(CW1.0873080213968933e\-11\fR.
.PP
\f(CW%zu\fR would have failed on MSVC, in two places silently
.IX Subsection "%zu would have failed on MSVC, in two places silently"
.PP
Nine plain \f(CW\*(C`snprintf\*(C'\fR calls used the C99 \f(CW%zu\fR length modifier, which MSVC\*(Aqs
older CRT does not implement — it prints the literal text. Two of the nine
build \fBhash keys\fR, not messages: \f(CW\*(C`csort\*(C'\fR\*(Aqs AoA → AoH and AoA → HoA
conversions name their columns \f(CW"0" .. "ncols\-1"\fR, so on such a build every
column would have been stored under the key \f(CW"zu"\fR and all but one lost. A
third is the \f(CW\*(C`Index N\*(C'\fR group label that \f(CW\*(C`oneway_test\*(C'\fR returns to the caller;
the rest are counts inside \f(CW\*(C`croak\*(C'\fR text. All 9 now use \f(CW\*(C`my_snprintf\*(C'\fR with
\&\f(CW\*(C`UVuf\*(C'\fR, the modifier Configure picks for the build\*(Aqs own \f(CW\*(C`UV\*(C'\fR.
.PP
\f(CW\*(C`ssize_t\*(C'\fR does not exist outside POSIX
.IX Subsection "ssize_t does not exist outside POSIX"
.PP
Seven declarations used the lowercase POSIX spelling. Perl never defines it —
there is no \f(CW\*(C`typedef\*(C'\fR or \f(CW\*(C`#define\*(C'\fR for it anywhere in the perl source, and
\&\f(CW\*(C`win32/win32.h\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`config_H.vc\*(C'\fR do not supply one either — so the file
compiled only because glibc\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`<sys/types.h>\*(C'\fR happens to. An MSVC build failed
at the first one. They are \f(CW\*(C`SSize_t\*(C'\fR now, or \f(CW\*(C`size_t\*(C'\fR where the value is a
length that cannot be negative, matching the 430 declarations that already
spelled it that way.
.PP
Two zero\-length calls on null pointers
.IX Subsection "Two zero-length calls on null pointers"
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`qsort\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`memcmp\*(C'\fR are both declared to take non\-null pointers whatever the
element count, so passing \f(CW\*(C`NULL\*(C'\fR is undefined even for zero elements: UBSan
reports it, and a compiler may infer non\-nullness and delete a later check.
\&\f(CW\*(C`p_adjust\*(C'\fR reached \f(CW\*(C`qsort(NULL, 0, …)\*(C'\fR whenever no row hash had any keys, and
\&\f(CW\*(C`drop_duplicates\*(C'\fR reached \f(CW\*(C`memcmp(NULL, NULL, 0)\*(C'\fR when every key seen so far had
been zero\-length. Both were reachable from one line of Perl —
\&\f(CW\*(C`p_adjust([{}, {}])\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`drop_duplicates([{}, {}])\*(C'\fR. The suite is now clean
under both UBSan and AddressSanitizer.
.PP
\f(CW\*(C`malloc\*(C'\fR/\f(CW\*(C`free\*(C'\fR in the \f(CW\*(C`write_table\*(C'\fR announcement
.IX Subsection "malloc/free in the write_table announcement"
.PP
The confirmation line allocated with libc\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`malloc\*(C'\fR when the path pushed it
past a 512\-byte stack buffer — the only two such calls in the file, where
everything else uses \f(CW\*(C`Newx\*(C'\fR/\f(CW\*(C`Safefree\*(C'\fR so that the module uses whatever
allocator its perl was built with, which on a \f(CW\*(C`\-Dusemymalloc\*(C'\fR perl is not
libc\*(Aqs. \f(CW\*(C`Newx\*(C'\fR is not the substitute here, since it croaks on failure and dying
inside the confirmation line after the table has been written is worse than
splitting the line; the long\-path case now takes the same three\-write path that
the old out\-of\-memory branch did.
.PP
\fIread_table\fR
.IX Subsection "read_table"
.PP
\f(CW\*(C`na.strings\*(C'\fR / \f(CW\*(C`na_values\*(C'\fR
.IX Subsection "na.strings / na_values"
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`read_table\*(C'\fR can now be told which field texts mean "missing". It is one option
under three names — R\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`na.strings\*(C'\fR, pandas\*(Aq \f(CW\*(C`na_values\*(C'\fR, and \f(CW\*(C`write_table\*(C'\fR\*(Aqs
own \f(CW\*(C`undef.val\*(C'\fR — so it can be spelled whichever way the surrounding code
already does. Each takes a string or an array reference, each means the same
thing, and passing more than one is an error the way \f(CW\*(C`sep\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`delim\*(C'\fR together
are:
.PP
.Vb 3
\& read_table(\*(Aqcohort.csv\*(Aq, \*(Aqna.strings\*(Aq => \*(AqNA\*(Aq);
\& read_table(\*(Aqcohort.csv\*(Aq, na_values    => [\*(AqNA\*(Aq, \*(AqN/A\*(Aq, \*(AqNULL\*(Aq, \*(Aq\-\*(Aq]);
\& read_table(\*(Aqcohort.csv\*(Aq, \*(Aqundef.val\*(Aq  => \*(AqNA\*(Aq);
.Ve
.PP
An empty field has always read as \f(CW\*(C`undef\*(C'\fR. Nothing else did, and there was no
way to ask for it, so a file could not be read back the way it had been
written: \f(CW\*(C`write_table\*(C'\fR renders an \f(CW\*(C`undef\*(C'\fR cell with \f(CW\*(C`undef.val\*(C'\fR, which 0.302
listed as a known limitation whose closing sentence proposed exactly this
option. The round trip now closes, and \f(CW\*(C`undef.val\*(C'\fR is accepted as the third
name so that both halves of it can use one spelling:
.PP
.Vb 2
\& write_table($rows, \*(Aqout.csv\*(Aq, \*(Aqundef.val\*(Aq => \*(AqNA\*(Aq);
\& my $back = read_table(\*(Aqout.csv\*(Aq, \*(Aqundef.val\*(Aq => \*(AqNA\*(Aq);   # undef again
.Ve
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`write_table\*(C'\fR\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`undef.val\*(C'\fR is a single token, being what it writes;
\&\f(CW\*(C`read_table\*(C'\fR\*(Aqs takes a list, being every token it should recognise.
.PP
The consequence had been quiet rather than loud. An unmapped \f(CW\*(C`NA\*(C'\fR is an
ordinary string, and Perl numifies a string to \f(CW0\fR, so it did not stop \f(CW\*(C`mean\*(C'\fR
or \f(CW\*(C`sd\*(C'\fR — it pulled them toward zero, warning once per cell on \f(CW\*(C`STDERR\*(C'\fR and
dying only under \f(CW\*(C`warnings FATAL => \*(Aqall\*(Aq\*(C'\fR. On the \f(CW\*(C`titanic.csv\*(C'\fR in the
repository root, whose 2207 rows carry 3690 literal \f(CW\*(C`NA\*(C'\fR cells across six
columns:
.PP
The mapped column is \f(CW\*(C`mean(d[[col]], na.rm=TRUE)\*(C'\fR in R 4.6.1 to every digit
shown, for all four.
.PP
Three choices are worth stating, because each follows R where pandas differs,
and each is asserted in the test rather than left to be discovered:
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBIt is off by default.\fR R\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`read.table\*(C'\fR defaults to \f(CW\*(C`na.strings = "NA"\*(C'\fR
and pandas recognises a 19\-token set, but \f(CW\*(C`read_table\*(C'\fR recognises nothing
beyond the empty field unless asked. A literal \f(CW\*(C`NA\*(C'\fR is real data in every
script written against an earlier release, and \f(CW\*(C`t/read_table.t\*(C'\fR pins that;
changing the default is a separate decision from adding the option.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBThe list replaces the set rather than extending one.\fR
\&\f(CW\*(Aqna.strings\*(Aq => \*(Aqbaz\*(Aq\fR maps \f(CW\*(C`baz\*(C'\fR and leaves \f(CW\*(C`NA\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR alone. pandas
adds \f(CW\*(C`baz\*(C'\fR to its defaults and maps all three.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBThe match is on the exact field text\fR, case\-sensitively and with no
whitespace stripped. \f(CW\*(Aq NA\*(Aq\fR is not \f(CW\*(AqNA\*(Aq\fR, and \f(CW\*(Aq\-999.000\*(Aq\fR is not
\&\f(CW\*(Aq\-999.0\*(Aq\fR — pandas numifies both sides and matches them; R compares the
string and does not, and neither does this.
.PP
The mapping happens where the empty\-field rule already did, so it reaches every
\&\f(CW\*(C`output.type\*(C'\fR, the \f(CW\*(C`.xlsx\*(C'\fR reader, and a value a \f(CW\*(C`filter\*(C'\fR writes back. The
header is never mapped, so a column may still be named \f(CW\*(C`NA\*(C'\fR. A \f(CW\*(C`filter\*(C'\fR runs
after the mapping and so sees \f(CW\*(C`undef\*(C'\fR rather than the token, which makes
\&\f(CW\*(C`sub { defined $_ }\*(C'\fR the way to keep the rows that have a value. For \f(CW\*(C`hoh\*(C'\fR, a
mapped row\-name cell is a missing row name and is refused exactly as an empty
one is.
.PP
Everything above is checked in the new \f(CW\*(C`t/read_table.na_strings.t\*(C'\fR (95 tests),
whose cases are taken from the reference suites rather than invented here: R\*(Aqs
\&\f(CW\*(C`tests/reg\-tests\-1a.R\*(C'\fR — the \f(CW\*(C`read.table(na.strings="foo")\*(C'\fR case at 1911\-1918
and PR#6781 at 2899\-2901 — and \f(CW\*(C`src/library/utils/man/read.table.Rd\*(C'\fR, plus
pandas 2.2.3\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`pandas/tests/io/parser/test_na_values.py\*(C'\fR (\f(CW\*(C`test_string_nas\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CW\*(C`test_detect_string_na\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`test_non_string_na_values\*(C'\fR for gh\-3611,
\&\f(CW\*(C`test_default_na_values\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`test_custom_na_values\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`test_na_trailing_columns\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CW\*(C`test_na_values_scalar\*(C'\fR for gh\-12224, and \f(CW\*(C`test_na_values_dict_aliasing\*(C'\fR). The
expected values are frozen literals with their provenance in the file header,
so the test needs no R and no Python to run. Three divergences from pandas are
asserted deliberately, the three above; a fourth is that a row short of the
header is still an alignment error naming the row rather than the NA padding
pandas does.
.PP
The full suite is 130 files and 26,444 tests, and \f(CW\*(C`./test.all.perls.pl\*(C'\fR passes
on all five local perls — \f(CW5.10.1\fR, \f(CW5.12.5\fR (long double), \f(CW5.42.3\fR, \f(CW5.44.0\fR
and \f(CW\*(C`5.44.0\-quadmath\*(C'\fR — with no warnings on any of them.
.PP
\fIDistribution functions\fR
.IX Subsection "Distribution functions"
.PP
Eight new functions — \f(CW\*(C`qnorm\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`pt\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`qt\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`pchisq\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`qchisq\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`pf\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`qf\*(C'\fR and
\&\f(CW\*(C`pbinom\*(C'\fR — with R\*(Aqs names, R\*(Aqs positional\-or\-named parameters and R\*(Aqs
\&\f(CW\*(C`lower.tail\*(C'\fR / \f(CW\*(C`log.p\*(C'\fR flags. Before this, \f(CW\*(C`pnorm\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`dnorm\*(C'\fR were the whole
family, so a Wald bound or a likelihood\-ratio test could not be finished from
outside the module: the critical value simply was not expressible. See
"Distribution functions" for the details; two are
worth pulling out here.
.PP
\&\fBNo tail is formed as \fR\f(CB\*(C`1 \-\*(C'\fR\fB the other one.\fR That subtraction discards
everything below machine epsilon, which is the range a p\-value is interesting
in, so each function is routed to the parameterisation that computes the
requested side directly — \f(CWpt(q)\fR is \f(CWpt_upper(\-q)\fR by symmetry, and \f(CW\*(C`pchisq\*(C'\fR
lower goes through a new \f(CWigam()\fR (the lower incomplete gamma that \f(CWigamc()\fR
had always formed internally and thrown away):
.PP
.Vb 1
\& pchisq(1e\-30, 1);     # 7.978845608028654e\-16, where 1 \- igamc gives 0
.Ve
.PP
\&\fR\f(CB\*(C`qf\*(C'\fR\fB disagrees with R in the far tail of \fR\f(BIF\fR\fB, and is right.\fR Arbitrated by
\&\f(CW\*(C`t/distributions.mpmath.py\*(C'\fR at \f(CW\*(C`mp.dps = 60\*(C'\fR, bisecting the defining equation
rather than calling a library inverse:
.PP
R\*(Aqs own \f(CW\*(C`pf\*(C'\fR settles it: \f(CW\*(C`pf\*(C'\fR of this module\*(Aqs answer returns the target
probability to 1.6e\-15, and \f(CW\*(C`pf\*(C'\fR of R\*(Aqs own \f(CW\*(C`qf\*(C'\fR answer returns it to 4.9e\-4.
It is R\*(Aqs inverse in that tail, not its CDF. Thirteen such rows are asserted
twice in \f(CW\*(C`t/distributions.R.scipy.t\*(C'\fR — right against the 60\-digit value, and
still disagreeing with R — so if a future R fixes \f(CW\*(C`qf\*(C'\fR, or this module
regresses onto R\*(Aqs answer, the row fails rather than quietly agreeing. Three
rows go the other way and are recorded the same way rather than tolerated.
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`log => 1\*(C'\fR on a \f(CW\*(C`p*\*(C'\fR function is \f(CWlog()\fR of the probability already computed,
not a log carried through the series, so a tail that rounded to \f(CW1\fR logs to \f(CW0\fR
where R reports a tiny negative number. Four of SciPy\*(Aqs six \f(CW\*(C`chi2\*(C'\fR log
references land in that case and are pinned as exactly \f(CW0\fR with the true value
named. \f(CW\*(C`ncp\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`qbinom\*(C'\fR are not implemented.
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`t/distributions.R.scipy.t\*(C'\fR is 2041 tests: a frozen table of R 4.6.1 values at
dyadic inputs (generated by the committed \f(CW\*(C`t/distributions.R.scipy.R\*(C'\fR), SciPy
1.18.0\*(Aqs own mpmath cases from \f(CW\*(C`TestChi2\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`TestStudentT\*(C'\fR, and R\*(Aqs own
round\-trip identities from \f(CW\*(C`tests/d\-p\-q\-r\-tests.R\*(C'\fR. Every tolerance is the
worst error actually measured, printed by the test\*(Aqs own diagnostic.
.PP
Ten figures now sit with the documentation — one per function, \f(CW\*(C`dnorm\*(C'\fR and
\&\f(CW\*(C`pnorm\*(C'\fR in their own sections and the eight new ones under
"What each one does, in one picture". Each
shades the region its function integrates and writes the integral it evaluates,
so the \f(CW\*(C`p*\*(C'\fR/\f(CW\*(C`q*\*(C'\fR pair reads as one identity answered from opposite ends: given
the boundary, return the area; given the area, return the boundary. \f(CW\*(C`pbinom\*(C'\fR is
drawn as bars with a summation sign rather than an integral, because a binomial
is discrete and drawing an integral over a histogram would misstate what it
computes. \f(CW\*(C`distribution.plots.pl\*(C'\fR regenerates all ten; like the other plot
scripts it is author\-only and \f(CW\*(C`[PruneFiles]\*(C'\fR keeps it out of a release.
.PP
Two portability problems surfaced while pinning this and are worth recording,
because neither is about the C code. Perl 5.10.1\*(Aqs string\-to\-number conversion
does not parse a 17\-digit literal exactly — it reads \f(CW0.9999847412109375\fR one
ulp out, and reflecting a probability onto \f(CW\*(C`1 \- p\*(C'\fR turns that ulp into 7.3e\-12
relative — and it reads \f(CW9.332636185032188789901e\-302\fR as
\&\f(CW8.999999999999996e\-302\fR, a 3.7% error. So every argument and every
exactly\-representable expected value in \f(CW\*(C`t/distributions.R.scipy.t\*(C'\fR is written
as a dyadic ratio, \f(CW\*(C`M/2^K\*(C'\fR, which rebuilds by integer division on any perl. And
where R\*(Aqs frozen value is \f(CW0\fR because a double underflowed, a long\-double or
\&\f(CW\*(C`_\|_float128\*(C'\fR build computes the real number instead — \f(CW\*(C`pbinom(0, 1000, 0.75)\*(C'\fR
really is \f(CW\*(C`0.25^1000\*(C'\fR = 8.7e\-603 — so the test accepts that as agreement with a
reference that could not go lower, rather than failing the wider build for being
right. The same applies to \f(CW\*(C`log => 1\*(C'\fR near a probability of 1, where the
resolvable accuracy is \f(CW\*(C`NV_EPSILON / |log p|\*(C'\fR and the tolerance is scaled off
measured machine epsilon accordingly.
.PP
\fIEvery confidence limit now comes from the same critical value\fR
.IX Subsection "Every confidence limit now comes from the same critical value"
.PP
Adding \f(CW\*(C`qnorm\*(C'\fR made a discrepancy visible that had always been there. Nine
places in \f(CW\*(C`LikeR.xs\*(C'\fR built their own \f(CW\*(C`z\*(C'\fR by calling \f(CWinverse_normal_cdf()\fR —
Moro\*(Aqs rational approximation, of which the file\*(Aqs own comment said:
\&\fI"good to about 1e\-9, which is fine for a confidence limit"\fR — while \f(CW\*(C`qnorm\*(C'\fR
went through the Newton\-polished \f(CWnormal_quantile_hp()\fR. Which function you asked
therefore decided how many digits you got, and a Wald bound written by hand
from \f(CW\*(C`qnorm\*(C'\fR differed from the one \f(CW\*(C`glm\*(C'\fR reported in the tenth digit.
.PP
It is one function now. \f(CWstd_qnorm()\fR seeds with Moro, polishes with Newton
against the \f(CW\*(C`erfc\*(C'\fR\-based normal CDF, and reflects a probability above \f(CW0.5\fR onto
\&\f(CW\*(C`1 \- p\*(C'\fR before inverting; \f(CW\*(C`glm\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`cor_test\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`prop_test\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`epi_2x2\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CW\*(C`cmh_test\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`roc\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`survfit\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`coxph\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`wilcox_test\*(C'\fR all call it, \f(CW\*(C`qnorm\*(C'\fR
\&\fIis\fR it, and two near\-duplicates are gone: \f(CWwilcox_qnorm()\fR in the XS, which was
the same Newton loop without the reflection, and the pure\-Perl \f(CW_qnorm()\fR
(Acklam\*(Aqs approximation, also \f(CW\*(C`~1e\-9\*(C'\fR) that \f(CW\*(C`cohen_d\*(C'\fR used for its interval.
.PP
Measured against \f(CW\*(C`mpmath\*(C'\fR at \f(CW\*(C`mp.dps = 60\*(C'\fR, bisecting \f(CW\*(C`erfc(\-z/sqrt(2))/2 = p\*(C'\fR
rather than calling a library inverse, worst relative error over conf.level
0.8 to 0.9999:
.PP
Seven orders of magnitude, and the result is closer to the truth than either
reference. Nothing in the existing suite failed: every one of these functions is
cross\-validated against R at tolerances that had room for \f(CW1e\-9\fR in them,
because they had to. What changed is that they no longer need it —
\&\f(CW\*(C`t/distributions.R.scipy.t\*(C'\fR\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`glm\*(C'\fR agreement check went from \f(CW5e\-9\fR, with its
comment explaining that the gap was the design, to bit\-identical.
.PP
The bug this exposed: \f(CW0.95\fR is a \f(CW\*(C`double\*(C'\fR
.IX Subsection "The bug this exposed: 0.95 is a double"
.PP
With the critical value down to 0.4 ulp, the next\-largest error in a confidence
limit turned out to be the confidence level. Eleven functions declared their
default as
.PP
.Vb 1
\& NV conf_level = 0.95;
.Ve
.PP
and \f(CW0.95\fR in C is a literal of type \f(CW\*(C`double\*(C'\fR, so on a long\-double or
\&\f(CW\*(C`_\|_float128\*(C'\fR perl what lands in the \f(CW\*(C`NV\*(C'\fR is \f(CW0.95\fR rounded to 53 bits — 2.2e\-17
from the \f(CW\*(C`NV\*(C'\fR nearest \f(CW0.95\fR. \f(CW\*(C`p = 1 \- (1 \- conf_level)/2\*(C'\fR inherits all of it,
the normal density at the 95% point is \f(CW0.0584\fR, and the critical value comes
out 3.8e\-16 wrong: about 1700 ulp of a long double, and the single largest error
in a 95% interval on those builds. \f(CW\*(C`fisher_test\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`binom_test\*(C'\fR had dodged
this for releases by parsing the literal through perl\*(Aqs own number parser; that
idiom is now the \f(CW\*(C`NV_CONF_95\*(C'\fR macro and all thirteen sites use it.
.PP
This was invisible while the critical value itself was only good to \f(CW1e\-9\fR.
It is the reason \f(CW\*(C`t/qnorm.crit.R.scipy.t\*(C'\fR freezes \f(CW\*(C`p\*(C'\fR as well as \f(CW\*(C`conf.level\*(C'\fR:
\&\f(CW\*(C`1 \- (1 \- 0.999)/2\*(C'\fR has to round, and it rounds differently at each NV width, so
a shared expected value is only meaningful if every build is asked the same
question.
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`t/qnorm.crit.R.scipy.t\*(C'\fR is 166 tests — the value itself against R 4.6.1,
SciPy 1.18.0 and the 60\-digit arbiter, and then the same value recovered out of
seven functions\*(Aq reported intervals at six confidence levels each. It also pins
what \f(CWinverse_normal_cdf()\fR alone returns and asserts it is nowhere near the
tolerance, so the file cannot pass by accident if the routing is ever undone.
.PP
\fIResult keys are dotted everywhere\fR
.IX Subsection "Result keys are dotted everywhere"
.PP
Every key of every returned hash now uses R\*(Aqs dotted spelling. Previously the
module answered to both conventions and it was impossible to guess which:
\&\f(CW\*(C`p_value\*(C'\fR from nine functions but \f(CW\*(C`p.value\*(C'\fR from two, \f(CW\*(C`conf_int\*(C'\fR from four but
\&\f(CW\*(C`conf.int\*(C'\fR from three, \f(CW\*(C`prop_test\*(C'\fR returning \f(CW\*(C`conf.int\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`p_value\*(C'\fR in the
same hash, and \f(CW\*(C`shapiro_test\*(C'\fR returning both spellings of its own p\-value.
.PP
\&\fBThis breaks code that reads the old names, and it is the only change here
that does.\fR \f(CW\*(C`$r\->{p_value}\*(C'\fR is now \f(CW\*(C`$r\->{\*(Aqp.value\*(Aq}\*(C'\fR — note the quotes, which a
dotted key requires. \f(CW\*(C`table_one\*(C'\fR\*(Aqs output column is renamed with everything
else.
.PP
\&\fBArgument names are untouched.\fR They are a different surface, where the
underscore spelling exists on purpose as a synonym for the dotted one, so
\&\f(CW\*(C`conf_level => 0.99\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`\*(Aqconf.level\*(Aq => 0.99\*(C'\fR both still work everywhere they
did, and \f(CW\*(C`wilcox_test(..., conf_int => 1)\*(C'\fR still asks for an interval. The
duplicate stores are gone: \f(CW\*(C`shapiro_test\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`kruskal_test\*(C'\fR each wrote their
p\-value twice under the two spellings and now write it once.
.PP
\fIDecorative comment rules removed\fR
.IX Subsection "Decorative comment rules removed"
.PP
1,495 lines that were nothing but a comment marker and a run of dashes, and 380
comments whose text trailed off into one, are gone from \f(CW\*(C`LikeR.xs\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CW\*(C`lib/Stats/LikeR.pm\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`t/\*(C'\fR. \f(CW\*(C`/* ===== WILCOXON EXACT NULL DISTRIBUTIONS =====\*(C'\fR
is now \f(CW\*(C`/* WILCOXON EXACT NULL DISTRIBUTIONS\*(C'\fR, which is what the house style in
\&\f(CW\*(C`CLAUDE.md\*(C'\fR asks for anyway. Nothing after \f(CW\*(C`_\|_DATA_\|_\*(C'\fR was touched, no POD
region, and no line holding a \f(CW\*(C`/*\*(C'\fR or \f(CW\*(C`*/\*(C'\fR was deleted, so no comment can have
been left unterminated. The suite is unchanged at 28,485 tests either side of it.
.SS "0.302 2026\-08\-22 CDT"
.IX Subsection "0.302 2026-08-22 CDT"
\&\f(CW\*(C`sum\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`min\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`max\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`mean\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`sd\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`var\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`quantile\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`cor\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`cov\*(C'\fR are
between 2.5 and 10.7 times faster, and four of the nine are now faster than R.
\&\f(CW\*(C`merge\*(C'\fR is between 1.9 and 5.3 times faster and now beats R\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`merge\*(C'\fR at every
size measured, and \f(CW\*(C`uniq\*(C'\fR is about four times faster at every size measured. Nothing any of them returns changes, except where it was wrong:
five bugs turned up while the measurements were being taken. One had been
hanging the interpreter, one had been segfaulting, and three had been quietly
returning the wrong frame for any input built on a tied array.
.PP
The distribution had also been building without an optimizer, which is where a
third of this came from and none of the credit does.
.PP
At \f(CW\*(C`n = 1,000,000\*(C'\fR, one column of \f(CW\*(C`rnorm\*(C'\fR values, best of seven, measured by
\&\f(CW\*(C`scale.pl\*(C'\fR against \f(CW\*(C`scale.R\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`scale.py\*(C'\fR on the same machine:
.PP
The ratios are better below a million, where the column still fits in cache. At
\&\f(CW\*(C`n = 100,000\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`sum\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`mean\*(C'\fR are 5.1 times faster than 0.301, \f(CW\*(C`sd\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`var\*(C'\fR
5.2, \f(CW\*(C`quantile\*(C'\fR 8.0, and \f(CW\*(C`sum\*(C'\fR overtakes R.
.PP
What is left between this and NumPy is not a constant factor waiting to be
found. A perl array is an array of \f(CW\*(C`SV*\*(C'\fR; NumPy holds a contiguous \f(CW\*(C`double[]\*(C'\fR
and runs SIMD over it. 1.2 ns per element is about four cycles — load the
pointer, load the flags, branch, load the \f(CW\*(C`NVX\*(C'\fR, add — and that is near the
floor for walking a million SVs. Closing it would need a packed\-buffer type
holding \f(CW\*(C`NV*\*(C'\fR directly, which is a different module rather than a tuning pass.
.PP
\fIThe module was being built without an optimizer\fR
.IX Subsection "The module was being built without an optimizer"
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`compile.sh\*(C'\fR ran \f(CW\*(C`perl Makefile.PL OPTIMIZE=\*(Aq\-Wall\*(Aq\*(C'\fR. \f(CW\*(C`OPTIMIZE\*(C'\fR \fIreplaces\fR
perl\*(Aqs own \f(CW$Config{optimize}\fR rather than adding to it, so that was not "the
usual flags plus a warning switch" — it was the whole optimize line, and every
build it made, including the one \f(CW\*(C`make install\*(C'\fR then installed, was compiled at
\&\f(CW\*(C`\-O0\*(C'\fR. \f(CW\*(C`test.all.perls.pl\*(C'\fR defaulted to the same string, so the entire support
matrix was built and timed that way too.
.PP
Both say \f(CW\*(C`\-O2 \-Wall\*(C'\fR now. On the same \f(CW\*(C`LikeR.c\*(C'\fR, summing \f(CW1e6\fR NVs takes 4.10 ms
at \f(CW\*(C`\-O0\*(C'\fR against 2.63 ms at \f(CW\*(C`\-O2\*(C'\fR, and \f(CW\*(C`cor\*(C'\fR 25.5 ms against 12.9 ms. Read the
rest of this section as what was left after the compiler was allowed to do its
job.
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`t/build.optimize.t\*(C'\fR reads both scripts and fails if either hands \f(CW\*(C`Makefile.PL\*(C'\fR
an \f(CW\*(C`OPTIMIZE\*(C'\fR with no \f(CW\*(C`\-O\*(C'\fR in it. There is no way to ask a loaded \f(CW\*(C`.so\*(C'\fR what it
was compiled at, so the check has to be on the scripts; neither ships, so the
file skips outside the repository.
.PP
\fIReading a column: one \fR\f(CI\*(C`av_fetch\*(C'\fR\fI per element\fR
.IX Subsection "Reading a column: one av_fetch per element"
.PP
All nine functions read their input by calling \f(CW\*(C`av_fetch\*(C'\fR once per element. That
call is out of line and repeats, for every element, a bounds check, a
negative\-index fixup and a magic dispatch that a straight walk of \f(CW\*(C`AvARRAY\*(C'\fR
already knows the answers to. On a million\-element column it was about half of
what \f(CW\*(C`sum\*(C'\fR cost and more than half of \f(CW\*(C`cor\*(C'\fR.
.PP
Walking the block directly is only sound while nothing can move it, and \f(CW\*(C`SvNV\*(C'\fR
can move it: on an SV carrying get magic it runs \f(CW\*(C`mg_get\*(C'\fR, on a reference it may
run an overloaded \f(CW\*(C`0+\*(C'\fR, and on a string that is not a number it raises a warning
that a \f(CW$SIG{_\|_WARN_\|_}\fR handler catches. Each of those is perl code, and perl
code may push to the very array being walked, which reallocs the block and
leaves the walk holding freed memory.
.PP
So the fast path is decided per element, not per array. \f(CW\*(C`sv_plain_nv\*(C'\fR takes an
SV only when it already holds a number and carries neither magic nor a
reference, which makes \f(CW\*(C`SvNV\*(C'\fR a register read that calls nothing; everything
else — a hole, a string, a reference, a tied element — goes back to \f(CW\*(C`av_fetch\*(C'\fR,
which is both correct and where the block pointer gets re\-derived. A tied array
is refused whole, before the scan starts.
.PP
The scans live in their own functions and are marked \f(CW\*(C`LIKER_NOINLINE\*(C'\fR, which is
not decoration. Inlined into the caller, a scan shares a stack frame with the
\&\f(CW\*(C`av_fetch\*(C'\fR loop that finishes whatever it would not take; the accumulators then
have to stay addressable across that call, so the scan spills them to memory
every iteration instead of keeping them in registers. Summing \f(CW1e6\fR NVs: 2.77
ns/element for the \f(CW\*(C`av_fetch\*(C'\fR loop being replaced, 2.25 with the scan inlined
into it, 1.29 with the scan compiled on its own. There is no portable spelling
of \f(CW\*(C`noinline\*(C'\fR, so it is annotated where the compiler has one and left empty
where it does not, which gives back the inlined speed and never a wrong answer.
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`min\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`max\*(C'\fR get a scan each rather than sharing one that tracks both ends.
The running extreme is a loop\-carried dependency, each compare\-and\-move waiting
on the previous element\*(Aqs result, so carrying the end the caller will not return
costs a second chain for nothing: 1.79 ms against 1.35 ms on \f(CW1e6\fR NVs.
.PP
\fR\f(CI\*(C`var\*(C'\fR\fI, \fR\f(CI\*(C`sd\*(C'\fR\fI and \fR\f(CI\*(C`cov\*(C'\fR\fI: Welford to two passes\fR
.IX Subsection "var, sd and cov: Welford to two passes"
.PP
Welford\*(Aqs recurrence updates the running mean with \f(CW\*(C`mean += delta / count\*(C'\fR. That
divide sits in a loop\-carried dependency chain, so each element waits out the
previous one\*(Aqs latency — 5.34 ns/element against 2.70 for two passes.
.PP
Two passes is also what R computes. \f(CW\*(C`src/library/stats/src/cov.c\*(C'\fR takes a first
mean, refines it with a second pass (\f(CW\*(C`tmp = tmp + sum(x \- tmp)/n\*(C'\fR, the \f(CW\*(C`MEAN\*(C'\fR
macro) and only then sums the squared deviations about the refined mean.
Expanding that third pass about the \fIunrefined\fR mean leaves
\&\f(CW\*(C`sum((x\-m)^2) \- (sum(x\-m))^2/n\*(C'\fR, so carrying the sum of the deviations alongside
the sum of their squares gives R\*(Aqs answer in one pass fewer. That is the
Chan\-Golub\-LeVeque correction, so it is no less accurate than Welford on an
ill\-centred column.
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`mean\*(C'\fR is deliberately not changed: it remains the single\-pass \f(CW\*(C`sum/n\*(C'\fR, which is
not what R\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`mean\*(C'\fR computes and never was.
.PP
Reading the arguments twice is visible only to a tied array, whose \f(CW\*(C`FETCH\*(C'\fR now
runs once per pass.
.PP
\fR\f(CI\*(C`quantile\*(C'\fR\fI orders only the statistics it reads\fR
.IX Subsection "quantile orders only the statistics it reads"
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`quantile\*(C'\fR sorted the whole column and then indexed into it. Type 7 reads at
most two order statistics per probability — \f(CW\*(C`x[j]\*(C'\fR at \f(CW\*(C`j = floor((n\-1)p)\*(C'\fR, and
\&\f(CW\*(C`x[j+1]\*(C'\fR where the interpolation weight is non\-zero — which for the five default
probs is ten values out of a million.
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`nv_select_multi\*(C'\fR places just those. It takes the middle index first and
recurses into the two ranges that index separates, costing \f(CW\*(C`O(n log k)\*(C'\fR rather
than the \f(CW\*(C`O(n k)\*(C'\fR a left\-to\-right sweep of selects would: each level of the
recursion touches each element at most once, and the index list halves at every
level. Past \f(CW\*(C`k = n/2\*(C'\fR distinct indices a full sort is the cheaper way to get the
same array, and that is what still happens there.
.PP
This is also what R does — \f(CW\*(C`quantile.default\*(C'\fR hands its indices to
\&\f(CWsort(partial=)\fR, and NumPy reaches \f(CW\*(C`np.partition\*(C'\fR for the same reason. On \f(CW1e6\fR
random doubles the ten order statistics of the five default probs cost 19.2 ms
this way against 69.1 ms for the full introsort.
.PP
The probabilities are parsed before the column is read now, rather than after it
is sorted, so an invalid \f(CW\*(C`probs\*(C'\fR vector no longer costs a pass over the data
first.
.PP
\fR\f(CI\*(C`cor\*(C'\fR\fI and \fR\f(CI\*(C`cov\*(C'\fR\fI: sorts that inline their comparison\fR
.IX Subsection "cor and cov: sorts that inline their comparison"
.PP
Spearman ranks its input before correlating it, and \f(CW\*(C`rank_data\*(C'\fR did that through
\&\f(CW\*(C`qsort\*(C'\fR with an out\-of\-line comparator; Kendall\*(Aqs tau\-b sorted three times the
same way. That comparator call cannot be inlined, and for a sort of a few
thousand elements it is most of what the sort costs — the module\*(Aqs own \f(CW\*(C`nv_sort\*(C'\fR
takes 210us where \f(CW\*(C`qsort\*(C'\fR takes 355us on 5,000 NVs, which is why it exists.
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`LIKER_DEFINE_SORT(T, PFX, LESS)\*(C'\fR generates that introsort for a given struct
and ordering: median of three left at the midpoint so both partition scans have
a sentinel, recursion into the shorter side and a loop on the longer so the
stack stays \f(CW\*(C`O(log n)\*(C'\fR deep, a heapsort fallback past \f(CW\*(C`2*floor(log2 n)\*(C'\fR levels
so a median\-of\-three killer cannot reach \f(CWO(n^2)\fR, and one insertion sort over
the nearly\-ordered remainder. It is \f(CW\*(C`kw_sort\*(C'\fR, the Kruskal\-Wallis sort, made
generic. \f(CW\*(C`rank_data\*(C'\fR and Kendall\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`(x, y)\*(C'\fR sort are generated from it; Kendall\*(Aqs
third sort, a plain \f(CW\*(C`NV\*(C'\fR column, calls \f(CW\*(C`nv_sort\*(C'\fR directly.
.PP
glibc\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`qsort\*(C'\fR is also a mergesort that allocates a scratch buffer the size of
the array it is given. Peak RSS for a Spearman correlation of two \f(CW2e6\fR columns
falls from 455 MB to 424 MB — exactly the 32 MB it was allocating to sort \f(CW2e6\fR
sixteen\-byte records.
.PP
At \f(CW\*(C`n = 200,000\*(C'\fR: \f(CW\*(C`cor(..., \*(Aqspearman\*(Aq)\*(C'\fR 66.9 ms to 27.2 ms, \f(CW\*(C`cov(...,
\&\*(Aqspearman\*(Aq)\*(C'\fR 65.9 ms to 26.7 ms, \f(CW\*(C`cor(..., \*(Aqkendall\*(Aq)\*(C'\fR 70.5 ms to 44.7 ms. Small
repeated calls gain the same factor: ten thousand \f(CW\*(C`cor(..., \*(Aqspearman\*(Aq)\*(C'\fR calls
over twelve elements, the shape \f(CW\*(C`agg\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`group_by\*(C'\fR produce, 6.4 ms to 3.1 ms.
.PP
\fR\f(CI\*(C`cor\*(C'\fR\fI read an array of arrays once per column\fR
.IX Subsection "cor read an array of arrays once per column"
.PP
\&\f(CWcor(\e@matrix)\fR extracted one column at a time, and for each column it walked
every row — \f(CW\*(C`av_fetch\*(C'\fR on the outer array, \f(CW\*(C`SvRV\*(C'\fR to reach the row, then
\&\f(CW\*(C`av_fetch\*(C'\fR for the cell. The outer array was therefore walked \f(CW\*(C`ncols\*(C'\fR times
over, and every one of the \f(CW\*(C`ncols * nrows\*(C'\fR cells paid a fetch on it that the
column before had already paid.
.PP
The pre\-validation pass that already checks every row is an array reference now
keeps the row \f(CW\*(C`AV*\*(C'\fRs it resolves instead of discarding them, and the extraction
transposes in a single pass over the rows through \f(CW\*(C`av_extract_or_nan\*(C'\fR — the same
reader the vector branch uses, so a short row, a hole, an \f(CW\*(C`undef\*(C'\fR and a
non\-numeric cell all behave here exactly as they do there. Outer fetches fall
from \f(CW\*(C`ncols * nrows\*(C'\fR to \f(CW\*(C`nrows\*(C'\fR, and each row\*(Aqs cells are read in the order they
are stored rather than one per pass.
.PP
At 20,000 rows: \f(CW\*(C`cor\*(C'\fR of a 40\-column matrix 40.6 ms to 26.7 ms, and its
cross\-correlation against an 8\-column matrix 28.7 ms to 12.4 ms. The transpose
keeps one write stream open per column, which is what a wide matrix pays for
this, and 2,000 x 300 still comes out ahead — 144.6 ms to 134.8 ms.
.PP
A row that was itself a tied array used to read as all\-\f(CW\*(C`NA\*(C'\fR, because \f(CW\*(C`av_fetch\*(C'\fR
on one hands back a deferred \f(CW\*(C`PVLV\*(C'\fR whose value only arrives when \f(CW\*(C`mg_get\*(C'\fR runs:
\&\f(CWcor(\e@rows)\fR croaked "standard deviation is 0 in x column 0" on data that was
perfectly well defined. \f(CW\*(C`av_extract_or_nan\*(C'\fR runs the get magic, so those rows
now give the same answer as the untied equivalent. A cell that is a tied scalar
was already read correctly and still is.
.PP
The zero\-variance check that used to be folded into the extraction is now
\&\f(CW\*(C`nv_all_equal\*(C'\fR, one function shared with the vector branch, which returns at the
first pair of values that differ instead of sweeping the column. On the vector
path that replaces two full passes over \f(CW\*(C`x\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`y\*(C'\fR, which is where the \f(CW\*(C`cor\*(C'\fR
row of the table above moves from 10.5 ms to 8.24 ms — the two passes were a
fifth of what \f(CW\*(C`cor(\e@x, \e@y)\*(C'\fR cost at \f(CW\*(C`n = 1,000,000\*(C'\fR.
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`cov\*(C'\fR does not gain from any of this: its Pearson path neither sorts nor scans
for zero variance, and 8.75 ms is the same figure it read before. What changed
there is housekeeping — \f(CW\*(C`Newx\*(C'\fR in place of a bare \f(CW\*(C`safemalloc\*(C'\fR, and the method
string resolved once rather than by three more \f(CW\*(C`strcmp\*(C'\fR calls.
.PP
\fR\f(CI\*(C`min\*(C'\fR\fI over more than 65,535 arguments never returned\fR
.IX Subsection "min over more than 65,535 arguments never returned"
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`min\*(C'\fR counted its arguments in an \f(CW\*(C`unsigned short int\*(C'\fR. \f(CW\*(C`items\*(C'\fR is the whole
flattened argument list, so \f(CWmin(@x)\fR on an array of more than 65,535 scalars
wrapped the counter and the loop never reached its bound:
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`perl
my @a = (1 .. 70_000);
min(@a);                  # 0.301: hangs, forever
\&\*(C'\fR
.PP
It is a \f(CW\*(C`Stack_off_t\*(C'\fR now, which is what \f(CW\*(C`max\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`sum\*(C'\fR already used.
\&\f(CW\*(C`power_t_test\*(C'\fR carried the same counter; it takes named pairs, so nothing could
reach 65,535 of them, and it is widened anyway.
.PP
A regression here is a hang rather than a wrong answer, so \f(CW\*(C`t/hot_path.t\*(C'\fR runs
the 70,000\-argument calls under \f(CW\*(C`alarm\*(C'\fR where the platform has one. Without that
guard a smoker would sit there instead of failing.
.PP
\fIA tied array read as all\-\fR\f(CI\*(C`undef\*(C'\fR
.IX Subsection "A tied array read as all-undef"
.PP
\&\f(CWsum(\e@tied)\fR croaked \f(CW\*(C`undefined value at array ref index 0\*(C'\fR, and so did every
other function here. \f(CW\*(C`av_fetch\*(C'\fR on a tied array does not return the element: it
returns a mortal \f(CW\*(C`PVLV\*(C'\fR that only acquires the element\*(Aqs value once \f(CW\*(C`mg_get\*(C'\fR has
run on it. The \f(CW\*(C`SvOK\*(C'\fR test came first, saw an empty \f(CW\*(C`PVLV\*(C'\fR, and reported every
element of every tied array as undefined. An ordinary array holding a tied
scalar failed the same way.
.PP
The slow path runs \f(CW\*(C`SvGETMAGIC\*(C'\fR before it looks now. This predates 0.302 and is
not fallout from the scan — but the scan is what made it worth finding, because
\&\f(CW\*(C`sv_plain_nv\*(C'\fR refuses every magical SV and so sends all of them down that one
path.
.PP
That slow path then had a bug of its own, and it was worse. \f(CW\*(C`av_slow_at\*(C'\fR held
the \f(CW\*(C`SV **\*(C'\fR that \f(CW\*(C`av_fetch\*(C'\fR returned — a pointer \fIinto\fR \f(CW\*(C`AvARRAY\*(C'\fR — ran the get
magic through it, and read it again afterwards. \f(CW\*(C`mg_get\*(C'\fR runs perl, and the
element in \f(CW\*(C`t/hot_path.t\*(C'\fR pushes 200 values onto the array being walked, which
reallocates that block: both reads after the magic were reads of freed memory.
It usually returned the right answer because the freed block usually still held
the old pointer, so the test that exists for precisely this case passed on four
of the five perls and, on the fifth, only failed when the suite was run four
perls at a time and something else had claimed the block —
\&\f(CW\*(C`sum\*(C'\fR returned 1085 instead of 10, once. Under \f(CW\*(C`valgrind\*(C'\fR it is an "invalid
read of size 8" on every perl, every time.
.PP
It loads the element into a local before running the magic now. The SV itself
does not move; only the array of pointers to it does, so a copy of the pointer
stays good where a second read through the slot does not. The same shape — take
\&\f(CW\*(C`SV **\*(C'\fR from \f(CW\*(C`av_fetch\*(C'\fR, run magic, dereference again — is still present in the
\&\f(CW\*(C`median\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`moment\*(C'\fR readers and in three places in \f(CW\*(C`transpose\*(C'\fR, and is the
same one\-line fix in each; those are untouched here.
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`uniq\*(C'\fR was missed by this pass and fixed later in the same release; see
"\f(CW\*(C`uniq\*(C'\fR: the same keys, without a perl hash" below.
.PP
\fR\f(CI\*(C`cor\*(C'\fR\fI returned \fR\f(CI\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR\fI on an off\-centre column\fR
.IX Subsection "cor returned NaN on an off-centre column"
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`pearson_corr\*(C'\fR computed the correlation from raw cross\-products,
\&\f(CW\*(C`(n*sxy \- sx*sy) / sqrt((n*sx2 \- sx^2) * (n*sy2 \- sy^2))\*(C'\fR. Each of those three
terms subtracts two nearly equal large numbers, so it loses a digit of the
answer for every digit by which the mean exceeds the spread. On a column with
mean \f(CW1e9\fR and a spread of \f(CW0.05\fR it does not merely lose precision:
\&\f(CW\*(C`n*sx2 \- sx*sx\*(C'\fR comes out negative, the square root is \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR, and \f(CW\*(C`cor\*(C'\fR returns
\&\f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR for an ordinary pair of vectors. At mean \f(CW1e6\fR it was wrong in the fifth
decimal place — \f(CW0.74945606763640826\fR against R\*(Aqs \f(CW0.74943997817595964\fR.
.PP
It centres first now, as R\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`cov.c\*(C'\fR does, with the same compensated two\-pass
correction \f(CW\*(C`var\*(C'\fR uses. R reports \f(CW0.063454463733801467\fR for the \f(CW1e9\fR case, and
so does this.
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`cov\*(C'\fR\*(Aqs Pearson branch was Welford\*(Aqs covariance recurrence, two divides deep in
the dependency chain; it is \f(CW\*(C`nv_cov2\*(C'\fR now, the two\-pass form, shared with the
Spearman branch.
.PP
\fIA leak on \fR\f(CI\*(C`cor\*(C'\fR\fI\*(Aqs matrix error path\fR
.IX Subsection "A leak on cor's matrix error path"
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`cor(\e@x, \e@y)\*(C'\fR with a \f(CW\*(C`y\*(C'\fR matrix whose rows are empty croaked "y matrix has
zero columns" without freeing the \f(CW\*(C`x\*(C'\fR columns it had already extracted. On a
40\-column matrix that is 40 buffers and their table per call: 20,000 such croaks
grew RSS by 44 MB. The branch\*(Aqs allocations are now released through one macro
that every croak path calls, and the row tables and the scratch row are freed as
soon as the last value has been read out of them rather than at the end.
.PP
The \f(CW\*(C`nrows < 2\*(C'\fR guard was missing the same frees, but it is unreachable — a
one\-row matrix has a constant column, so the zero\-variance croak above always
gets there first. It frees anyway.
.PP
Seven \f(CW\*(C`croak\*(C'\fR formats in \f(CW\*(C`cor\*(C'\fR passed a \f(CW\*(C`size_t\*(C'\fR to \f(CW%lu\fR with no cast, which on
Windows is a 64\-bit argument read through a 32\-bit conversion. They use
\&\f(CW\*(C`%" UVuf "\*(C'\fR with a \f(CW\*(C`(UV)\*(C'\fR cast now, as the rest of the file does, and \f(CW\*(C`cov\*(C'\fR\*(Aqs
one — cast to \f(CW\*(C`unsigned long\*(C'\fR, so well defined but still narrowing on Win64 —
went with them. Every message is unchanged wherever \f(CW\*(C`unsigned long\*(C'\fR is 64 bits,
which is everywhere the suite runs.
.PP
\fIUnchanged: \fR\f(CI\*(C`cor\*(C'\fR\fI and \fR\f(CI\*(C`cov\*(C'\fR\fI still do not see an overloaded object as a number\fR
.IX Subsection "Unchanged: cor and cov still do not see an overloaded object as a number"
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`cor\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`cov\*(C'\fR decide what is numeric with \f(CW\*(C`looks_like_number\*(C'\fR, which is false
for any reference, an overloaded \f(CW\*(C`0+\*(C'\fR included, so a column of overloaded
objects reads as all\-\f(CW\*(C`NA\*(C'\fR: \f(CW\*(C`cov\*(C'\fR returns \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`cor\*(C'\fR reaches its
zero\-variance croak. \f(CW\*(C`sum\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`min\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`max\*(C'\fR do honour the overload, through
\&\f(CW\*(C`SvNV\*(C'\fR. The two families genuinely disagree. That predates 0.302 and is a
different question from how a column is walked, so \f(CW\*(C`t/hot_path.t\*(C'\fR pins the
current behaviour rather than changing it quietly.
.PP
\fR\f(CI\*(C`merge\*(C'\fR\fI: a hash join that stopped building a perl hash\fR
.IX Subsection "merge: a hash join that stopped building a perl hash"
.PP
At \f(CW\*(C`n = 300,000\*(C'\fR a \f(CW\*(C`merge($df, $df, how => \*(Aqinner\*(Aq, on => \*(Aqid\*(Aq)\*(C'\fR over a
six\-column HoA took 575 ms. It takes 109 ms. Best of ten, one process per
measurement, pinned to one CPU, against \f(CW\*(C`scale.R\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`scale.py\*(C'\fR on the same
machine:
.PP
Under callgrind the 30,000\-row join fell from 358M instructions to 170M. Three
things were paying for that.
.PP
\&\fBThe right\-hand index was a perl \fR\f(CB\*(C`HV\*(C'\fR keyed by the join key: an \f(CW\*(C`HE\*(C'\fR, a
shared \f(CW\*(C`HEK\*(C'\fR and an \f(CW\*(C`IV\*(C'\fR SV per distinct key, so three allocations per right row
whenever the key is unique, which is exactly what a join on an id column is.
It is the same open\-addressed table over a key arena that \f(CW\*(C`drop_duplicates\*(C'\fR
already interns into (\f(CW\*(C`dd_ctx\*(C'\fR) — no SV, no HEK, one \f(CW\*(C`memcpy\*(C'\fR of each distinct
key — so the two functions now share their definition of "the same key" as well
as their code for deciding it. \f(CW\*(C`mg_key\*(C'\fR writes straight into that arena instead
of making four \f(CW\*(C`sv_catpvn\*(C'\fR calls per key column into a scratch SV, and a
one\-column join writes the cell\*(Aqs bytes bare: with a single field there is
nothing for a length prefix to disambiguate, and on an integer id the prefix and
its two separators were about as many bytes again to hash and to compare.
.PP
\&\fBIt emitted each output row as it found it\fR, so the row count was not known
until the join had finished and every output column grew by \f(CW\*(C`av_push\*(C'\fR, being
reallocated its way up to the answer. The probe now records \f(CW\*(C`(left row, right
row)\*(C'\fR pairs into a flat list and builds nothing; only when that list is complete
— so the count is exact — is each column allocated once at its final size and
filled straight into \f(CW\*(C`AvARRAY\*(C'\fR, the way \f(CW\*(C`filter\*(C'\fR already builds its columns.
The list costs two \f(CW\*(C`SSize_t\*(C'\fR per output \fIrow\fR against roughly one SV per output
\&\fIcell\fR, so it is a small fraction of the result on any join wide enough to care,
a cross join included. \f(CW\*(C`mg_emit\*(C'\fR is gone; \f(CW\*(C`mg_column\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`mg_build\*(C'\fR replace it,
and the cross join goes through the same two stages as every other \f(CW\*(C`how\*(C'\fR.
.PP
\&\fR\f(CB\*(C`mg_cell\*(C'\fR\fB called \fR\f(CB\*(C`av_fetch\*(C'\fR\fB once per cell\fR, which was 7% of the call: a join
reads every key cell of both frames and every cell of every column it keeps.
It reads the block directly now, through the same \f(CW\*(C`av_at\*(C'\fR the other frame
functions use.
.PP
What is left is the result itself. A 300,000\-row join of two six\-column frames
is 3.3 million output SVs, and at 109 ms that is 33 ns each — allocate, copy a
cell into it, store it. pandas is not doing the same work: its columns are
contiguous typed buffers and a join is a \f(CW\*(C`take\*(C'\fR over them.
.PP
\fR\f(CI\*(C`uniq\*(C'\fR\fI: the same keys, without a perl hash\fR
.IX Subsection "uniq: the same keys, without a perl hash"
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`uniq\*(C'\fR on a million\-element column of \f(CW\*(C`rnorm\*(C'\fR values took 0.80 s, against R\*(Aqs
\&\f(CWunique()\fR at 0.019 and pandas\*(Aq \f(CW\*(C`pd.unique()\*(C'\fR at 0.029. It takes 0.16 s. Same
method as the tables above — one process per measurement, pinned to one CPU,
the fastest of seven, \f(CW\*(C`plot.scaling.pl\*(C'\fR against \f(CW\*(C`scale.R\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`scale.py\*(C'\fR on the
same machine — except that the \f(CW0.301\fR column is the 0.301 \fIcode\fR built at \f(CW\*(C`\-O2\*(C'\fR,
not the released build, so this is the rewrite on its own and not the optimizer
again:
.PP
The slope was never the problem. Fitted over the ladder by \f(CW\*(C`scaling.slopes.tsv\*(C'\fR,
all three are linear — 1.12 for \f(CW\*(C`uniq\*(C'\fR, 1.11 for R, 1.04 for pandas, and 1.14
for \f(CW\*(C`uniq\*(C'\fR before the rewrite — so this was a constant factor from the start,
and none of it was the hashing.
.PP
\&\fR\f(CB\*(C`SvPV\*(C'\fR\fB on an \fR\f(CB\*(C`NV\*(C'\fR\fB is a \fR\f(CB\*(C`%.15g\*(C'\fR\fB that has to be redone every time.\fR Walking a
million \f(CW\*(C`NV\*(C'\fR SVs costs 2 ms; walking them and calling \f(CW\*(C`SvPV\*(C'\fR on each costs
268 ms. That is ten times R\*(Aqs whole runtime for the same call, and it is the
floor the old code could not get under however few distinct values there were:
a column of a million elements holding ten distinct values still took 0.226 s.
Worse, \f(CW\*(C`SvPV\*(C'\fR leaves the rendered buffer on the caller\*(Aqs own SV without ever
reusing it (\f(CW\*(C`sv_2pv_flags\*(C'\fR re\-renders an \f(CW\*(C`NV\*(C'\fR on the next pass regardless), so
asking a large numeric column for its distinct values grew that column by tens
of megabytes, for good. \f(CW\*(C`nk_num_pv\*(C'\fR — already written for \f(CW\*(C`drop_duplicates\*(C'\fR,
about four times faster and only taken where the answer is provably the same —
renders into the XSUB\*(Aqs own stack buffer and touches nothing.
.PP
\&\fBThe \fR\f(CB\*(C`seen\*(C'\fR\fB hash was a perl \fR\f(CB\*(C`HV\*(C'\fR\fB.\fR An \f(CW\*(C`HE\*(C'\fR and a copied \f(CW\*(C`HEK\*(C'\fR per distinct
key is ~72 MB of scattered small allocations at a million of them, and the
table rehashes at every doubling on the way there. Going from ten distinct
values to a million added 0.47 s of pure insert cost. It is \f(CW\*(C`dd_ctx\*(C'\fR now, the
same open\-addressed slot array over a key arena that \f(CW\*(C`drop_duplicates\*(C'\fR and
\&\f(CW\*(C`merge\*(C'\fR intern into, presized from the element count.
.PP
\&\fBIt hashed each key twice\fR, once for \f(CW\*(C`hv_exists\*(C'\fR and again for \f(CW\*(C`hv_store\*(C'\fR.
Collapsing that into one \f(CW\*(C`hv_fetch(..., 1)\*(C'\fR is the obvious repair and is the
wrong one: the lvalue fetch mints an SV per key, and on the million distinct
doubles it measured 0.919 s against the pair\*(Aqs 0.756 — while also reading
\&\f(CW\*(C`AvARRAY\*(C'\fR directly, so the comparison flatters it. That is why the pair had
survived. One hash of one key into one open\-addressed probe is what the arena
gives instead.
.PP
What the rewrite does \fInot\fR do is compare doubles by value. That is how R and
pandas get the factor of six to eight they still have, and it is a different
answer:
\&\f(CW\*(C`0.1 + 0.2\*(C'\fR and \f(CW0.3\fR are two doubles that print the same, so they are one
value to \f(CW\*(C`uniq\*(C'\fR and two to \f(CWunique()\fR. \f(CW\*(C`uniq\*(C'\fR is documented to compare the way
\&\f(CW\*(C`eq\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`List::Util::uniq\*(C'\fR do, so it renders every element and compares the
text; that rendering pass is essentially all of the distance that is left.
Scalar context builds no result list at all and takes about a fifth off again.
.PP
\&\f(CWuniq(\e@tied)\fR croaked \f(CW\*(C`undefined value at array ref index 0\*(C'\fR. It was reading
elements through \f(CW\*(C`av_fetch\*(C'\fR and testing \f(CW\*(C`SvOK\*(C'\fR on the \f(CW\*(C`PVLV\*(C'\fR that comes back,
which is the bug "A tied array read as all\-\f(CW\*(C`undef\*(C'\fR" describes above. \f(CW\*(C`uniq\*(C'\fR was
simply not one of the functions that pass reached. It reads through
\&\f(CW\*(C`av_slow_at\*(C'\fR now, like the rest.
.PP
\fR\f(CI\*(C`filter\*(C'\fR\fI: measured, and left alone\fR
.IX Subsection "filter: measured, and left alone"
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`filter\*(C'\fR is not faster, and the reason is worth writing down so it is not
looked for again. At \f(CW\*(C`n = 300,000\*(C'\fR on a five\-column HoA, \f(CW\*(C`col(\*(Aqx\*(Aq) > 0\*(C'\fR keeping
half the rows costs 22.5 ms, of which \fBthe predicate is 1.3 ms\fR. The other
21.2 ms is allocating and filling the 750,000 SVs of the result, which the
documented contract requires: an HoA input, or any \f(CW\*(C`hoa\*(C'\fR output, builds fresh
arrays and fresh cell values. That is 19 ns per numeric cell and 44 ns per
string cell, and the difference between the two is one \f(CW\*(C`malloc\*(C'\fR for the string\*(Aqs
buffer.
.PP
Copy\-on\-write is the obvious way out of that \f(CW\*(C`malloc\*(C'\fR and is not available.
\&\f(CW\*(C`newSVsv\*(C'\fR is \f(CW\*(C`newSVsv_flags(sv, SV_GMAGIC|SV_NOSTEAL)\*(C'\fR, which does not pass
\&\f(CW\*(C`SV_COW_SHARED_HASH_KEYS\*(C'\fR, so its string copies are never copy\-on\-write; and
\&\f(CW\*(C`SV_DO_COW_SVSETSV\*(C'\fR, the flag pair a plain \f(CW\*(C`my $b = $a\*(C'\fR gets, is defined as \f(CW0\fR
unless \f(CW\*(C`PERL_CORE\*(C'\fR is set — perl\*(Aqs own \f(CW\*(C`sv.h\*(C'\fR says "the core is safe for this
COW optimisation, XS code on CPAN may not be". Reaching past that by hand was
measured anyway and is slower in both directions: \f(CW\*(C`filter\*(C'\fR 23.0 ms against 28.3
ms, \f(CW\*(C`merge\*(C'\fR 103 ms against 126 ms. For a string as short as a data frame\*(Aqs
usually are, the \f(CW\*(C`malloc\*(C'\fR costs less than what COW puts in its place — an
out\-of\-line \f(CW\*(C`sv_setsv\*(C'\fR, the \f(CW\*(C`CowREFCNT\*(C'\fR increment, and the read\-only flip on the
source. The finding is recorded at \f(CW\*(C`flt_cell_copy\*(C'\fR rather than only here.
.PP
The one lever that does move it is the copy itself. Sharing the surviving cells
instead — one refcount bump, which is what \f(CW\*(C`drop_duplicates\*(C'\fR does for exactly
this reason — was prototyped and measured at 13.6 ms against R\*(Aqs 14.4 ms. It is
not done, because \f(CW\*(C`filter\*(C'\fR documents the opposite and someone may be relying on
it; changing that is a decision about the interface, not a tuning pass.
.PP
Two things did change. \f(CW\*(C`flt_num\*(C'\fR takes a bare \f(CW\*(C`IOK\*(C'\fR/\f(CW\*(C`NOK\*(C'\fR cell without calling
\&\f(CW\*(C`SvGETMAGIC\*(C'\fR or \f(CW\*(C`looks_like_number\*(C'\fR — neither can say anything about an SV that
already holds a number, and \f(CW\*(C`looks_like_number\*(C'\fR is out of line — which is most
of what the predicate pass costs on a numeric column. And the tied\-frame bugs
below.
.PP
\fIA tied frame read as all\-\fR\f(CI\*(C`undef\*(C'\fR\fI, and one that segfaulted\fR
.IX Subsection "A tied frame read as all-undef, and one that segfaulted"
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`filter\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`merge\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`drop_duplicates\*(C'\fR all walk a column or a row through
\&\f(CW\*(C`AvARRAY\*(C'\fR, which is why they are fast and which is wrong for a tied array: its
elements do not exist until \f(CW\*(C`FETCH\*(C'\fR has run, and \f(CW\*(C`AvARRAY\*(C'\fR on one is not the
block they live in — on an array that has never held a real element it is a null
pointer. \f(CW\*(C`av_fetch\*(C'\fR is the way in, and what it returns for a tied element is a
mortal \f(CW\*(C`PVLV\*(C'\fR that only acquires the value once \f(CW\*(C`mg_get\*(C'\fR has run on it, so an
\&\f(CW\*(C`SvOK\*(C'\fR or \f(CW\*(C`SvROK\*(C'\fR test placed before that says "undef" or "not a reference" for
every cell and every row of the frame.
.PP
This is the same pair of mistakes \f(CWsum(\e@tied)\fR made, above, and it predates
0.302 in all three functions. Between them they were wrong in four distinct
ways:
.PP
.Vb 2
\& tie my @x, \*(AqTiedArray\*(Aq; @x = (1, \-2, 3);
\& tie my @y, \*(AqTiedArray\*(Aq; @y = (10, 20, 30);
\&
\& filter({ x => \e@x, y => \e@y }, col(\*(Aqx\*(Aq) > 0);
\& # 0.301: { x => [], y => [] } \-\- an empty frame, whatever the predicate
\& # 0.302: { x => [1, 3], y => [10, 30] }
\&
\& merge({ id => \e@x }, { id => [1, 3], w => [\*(Aqa\*(Aq,\*(Aqb\*(Aq] }, how => \*(Aqinner\*(Aq, on => \*(Aqid\*(Aq);
\& # 0.301: { id => [], w => [] } \-\- no key matched, because every key read undef
\& # 0.302: { id => [1, 3], w => [\*(Aqa\*(Aq, \*(Aqb\*(Aq] }
\&
\& drop_duplicates({ k => \e@x, v => \e@y });
\& # 0.301: { k => [undef], v => [undef] } \-\- every row identical, so one survived
\& # 0.302: { k => [1, \-2, 3], v => [10, 20, 30] }
\&
\& drop_duplicates([ map { { k => $_ } } 1 .. 3 ]);   # with the AoH itself tied
\& # 0.301: segmentation fault
.Ve
.PP
The crash was \f(CW\*(C`_aoh_key_union\*(C'\fR, which indexed \f(CW\*(C`AvARRAY\*(C'\fR with no bounds check and
no test for a tied array at all; on a tied AoH that is a null dereference on the
first row. An outer join was the quietest of the four — with no key matching, it
returned the two frames as disjoint halves, well\-formed and entirely wrong.
.PP
There is one reader now. \f(CW\*(C`av_at\*(C'\fR returns an element as it is — block read where
that is sound, \f(CW\*(C`av_fetch\*(C'\fR where the array is tied, and the pointer derived per
element rather than hoisted, because copying a cell can run perl and perl can
push to the array being walked. \f(CW\*(C`av_ref_at\*(C'\fR adds the \f(CW\*(C`mg_get\*(C'\fR and the
"is it a reference to the right thing" test for a row; \f(CW\*(C`av_row_keep\*(C'\fR is how a
row gets into a result, handing back the caller\*(Aqs own reference for a plain
array and a fresh reference to the same row for a tied one, since the \f(CW\*(C`PVLV\*(C'\fR is
mortal and stays bound to the tie. \f(CW\*(C`dd_cell\*(C'\fR runs the get magic before it looks
at the cell. None of it is measurable: \f(CW\*(C`drop_duplicates\*(C'\fR is within 2% of 0.301
on all three shapes at 30,000 and 300,000 rows, and \f(CW\*(C`merge\*(C'\fR\*(Aqs numbers above are
with it.
.PP
One consequence is visible and is documented under \f(CW\*(C`drop_duplicates\*(C'\fR: a tied HoA
column\*(Aqs cells cannot be \fIshared\fR into the result, because the tie has no cell
SV to share, so they are copied. It is the only place the "what survives is
shared" rule cannot hold, and copying is the only reading of it that can be
true.
.PP
A tied \fIframe\fR hash — the outer hash of a HoA or HoH, rather than a column or a
row inside it — is still not supported by any of the three. All of them read
its shape and its columns through \f(CW\*(C`HeVAL(hv_iternext(...))\*(C'\fR, which for a tied
hash is not the value. That is a larger change than this one and is not made
here; \f(CW\*(C`t/tied.frames.t\*(C'\fR says so where it stops.
.PP
\fITests\fR
.IX Subsection "Tests"
.PP
Three new test files, and the suite is 129 files and 26,331 tests.
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`t/var_sd_cov.R.t\*(C'\fR (162 tests) cross\-validates \f(CW\*(C`sum\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`min\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`max\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`mean\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CW\*(C`var\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`sd\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`cov\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`cor\*(C'\fR against R 4.6.1 over ten columns chosen to separate
the algorithms rather than to be representative: an arithmetic ladder, a
constant column, \f(CW\*(C`n = 2\*(C'\fR, mixed signs, and the column with mean \f(CW1e9\fR and spread
\&\f(CW0.05\fR that breaks a naive variance and did break the old \f(CW\*(C`cor\*(C'\fR. The expected
values are frozen literals with their provenance in the file header; the
generator, \f(CW\*(C`t/var_sd_cov.R.R\*(C'\fR, is committed beside it, and the test itself never
calls R.
.PP
Every value in the corpus is a dyadic rational, so the vectors are the same
numbers at every NV width, and the corpus is rebuilt in perl rather than pasted
in — the \f(CW\*(C`n\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`sum\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`min\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`max\*(C'\fR columns are what pin the perl construction
to R\*(Aqs. Those frozen numbers are printed \f(CW\*(C`%.40g\*(C'\fR, the double\*(Aqs exact decimal
expansion, because 17 significant digits round\-trip a double back to a double
and no further: a long\-double perl reads \f(CW100000000004.83398\fR as a different
number from the \f(CW100000000004.833984375\fR R had, which was enough to fail an
exact comparison on two of the five perls. The tolerance on the exact columns is
two ulps of whatever NV the running perl was built with, found by bisection at
run time rather than hardcoded, because perl\*(Aqs string\-to\-NV conversion is not
correctly rounded on a long\-double build — \f(CW\*(C`perl\-5.12.5\*(C'\fR reads that literal one
ulp short.
.PP
Every case also runs through a tied array, which cannot take the scan and has to
reach the same answer through \f(CW\*(C`av_fetch\*(C'\fR. That is the assertion that the fast
path and the slow path are the same function.
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`t/hot_path.t\*(C'\fR (135 tests) covers how the input is read rather than what comes
out: the 70,000\-argument counter; every kind of element the scan must refuse —
IV, UV above \f(CW\*(C`IV_MAX\*(C'\fR, NV, string, \f(CW\*(C`0+\*(C'\fR overload, tied element, and one magical
element in the middle of an otherwise plain column; holes and \f(CW\*(C`undef\*(C'\fR in all
three of their documented behaviours; and an element whose get magic pushes 200
values onto the array it lives in while that array is being walked. \f(CW\*(C`cor\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`cov\*(C'\fR
and \f(CW\*(C`var\*(C'\fR are checked for invariance under a location shift of \f(CW1e3\fR, \f(CW1e6\fR and
\&\f(CW1e9\fR, which is the property the old correlation failed, and \f(CW\*(C`quantile\*(C'\fR\*(Aqs
partial sort is checked against a full sort at every rank on a column with ties.
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`t/tied.frames.t\*(C'\fR (81 tests) covers \f(CW\*(C`filter\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`merge\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`drop_duplicates\*(C'\fR
over frames built on tied arrays and tied row hashes: every output shape, every
\&\f(CW\*(C`how\*(C'\fR, every \f(CW\*(C`keep\*(C'\fR, single and composite keys, an empty tied frame of each
shape, and the sharing rule on both sides of its one exception. Every case runs
the same call over tied input and over an identical plain copy and requires the
two answers to be equal — a fixed expected value would pin the plain answer as
well, which \f(CW\*(C`t/filter.t\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`t/merge.t\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`t/drop_duplicates.t\*(C'\fR already do, and
what has to be pinned here is that the two routes agree. \f(CW\*(C`t/merge.t\*(C'\fR\*(Aqs existing
reference join — plain Perl, run over all six input/output shape combinations —
is what checks that the rewritten join still means what it meant.
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`./test.all.perls.pl\*(C'\fR passes on all five local perls — \f(CW5.10.1\fR, \f(CW5.12.5\fR
(long double), \f(CW5.42.3\fR, \f(CW5.44.0\fR and \f(CW\*(C`5.44.0\-quadmath\*(C'\fR — with no warnings on
any of them, and \f(CW\*(C`LikeR.c\*(C'\fR compiles clean under \f(CW\*(C`\-std=c99 \-O2 \-Wall\*(C'\fR against
every one of their \f(CW\*(C`CORE\*(C'\fR directories.
.SS "0.301 2026\-08\-21 CDT"
.IX Subsection "0.301 2026-08-21 CDT"
there are numerous additions of \f(CW\*(C`restrict\*(C'\fR keywords, which may or may not improve speed
.PP
\fIkruskal_test\fR
.IX Subsection "kruskal_test"
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`kruskal_test\*(C'\fR cross\-validated against R 4.6.1\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`stats::kruskal.test()\*(C'\fR and
SciPy 1.18.0\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`TestKruskal\*(C'\fR, driven by those suites\*(Aq own cases rather than by
cases invented here. Six bugs are fixed: five in how the arguments and the data
are read before any ranking happens, and one in the chi\-squared tail, which
reaches every function that uses it. The test now needs a third of the memory
and runs in under half the time, and it returns the same answer twice in a row,
which it did not before.
.PP
Everything below is checked in the new \f(CW\*(C`t/kruskal_test.R.scipy.t\*(C'\fR (870 tests),
whose expected values are frozen literals with their provenance recorded in the
file header; it needs no R and no Python to run. The generator that produced
them, \f(CW\*(C`t/kruskal_test.R.scipy.R\*(C'\fR, is committed beside it. There had been no
\&\f(CW\*(C`t/kruskal_test.t\*(C'\fR at all — the only coverage was six assertions in \f(CW\*(C`t/01.t\*(C'\fR on
the single Hollander & Wolfe example, and none of the six bugs would have shown
up in it. The full suite is 125 files and 25,951 tests, and
\&\f(CW\*(C`./test.all.perls.pl\*(C'\fR passes on all five local perls — \f(CW5.10.1\fR, \f(CW5.12.5\fR
(long double), \f(CW5.42.3\fR, \f(CW5.44.0\fR and \f(CW\*(C`5.44.0\-quadmath\*(C'\fR — with no warnings on
any of them.
.PP
NaN was ranked instead of dropped
.IX Subsection "NaN was ranked instead of dropped"
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`looks_like_number\*(C'\fR is true for \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR, so a \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR went into the ranking. R
treats \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR as \f(CW\*(C`NA\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`complete.cases(x, g)\*(C'\fR removes it before \f(CWrank()\fR ever
sees it:
.PP
It also handed \f(CW\*(C`cmp_nv3\*(C'\fR a comparison that is never true for any pair
involving the \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR, which leaves \f(CW\*(C`qsort\*(C'\fR without the strict weak ordering it is
entitled to — the same defect \f(CW\*(C`wilcox_test\*(C'\fR had fixed in 0.298, where the
comment describing it is still in the file. \f(CW\*(C`+Inf\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`\-Inf\*(C'\fR are neither \f(CW\*(C`NA\*(C'\fR
nor \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR to R and a rank test has no trouble with them, so they are still kept
and ranked; that is now pinned rather than incidental.
.PP
The bad value propagated: \f(CW\*(C`table_one\*(C'\fR\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`_t1_cont_p\*(C'\fR hands its groups straight
to \f(CW\*(C`kruskal_test\*(C'\fR, so a single \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR among nine observations was reported at
\&\f(CW\*(C`p = 0.0273\*(C'\fR where dropping it gives \f(CW0.0439\fR.
.PP
A group with no data inflated the degrees of freedom
.IX Subsection "A group with no data inflated the degrees of freedom"
.PP
The hash\-of\-arrays form counted every key in \f(CW\*(C`k\*(C'\fR, including a key whose array
was empty or whose every element had been dropped. On
\&\f(CW\*(C`{a => [1,1,1], b => [2,2,2], c => []}\*(C'\fR that gave \f(CW\*(C`df = 2\*(C'\fR and
\&\f(CW\*(C`p = 0.0820849986238988\*(C'\fR for what is a two\-group problem with
\&\f(CW\*(C`df = 1, p = 0.025347318677468304\*(C'\fR. Such a group was already skipped when
forming the statistic and when building \f(CW\*(C`group.stats\*(C'\fR; only \f(CW\*(C`df\*(C'\fR still counted
it.
.PP
R refuses this case outright — \f(CW\*(C`all groups must contain data\*(C'\fR — and so does
\&\f(CW\*(C`kruskal_test\*(C'\fR now, because the alternative is to test the groups that do have
data under a \f(CW\*(C`df\*(C'\fR that counts one that does not. R\*(Aqs order of checks came with
it: it filters each group, refuses an empty one, and only then counts what is
left, so \f(CW\*(C`{a => [], b => []}\*(C'\fR is \f(CW\*(C`all groups must contain data\*(C'\fR and not
\&\f(CW\*(C`not enough observations\*(C'\fR. SciPy takes the other side of this and returns \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR
with a \f(CW\*(C`SmallSampleWarning\*(C'\fR; the divergence is recorded in the test file rather
than papered over. The \f(CW\*(C`x\*(C'\fR/\f(CW\*(C`g\*(C'\fR form cannot reach any of this — it mints a group
id the first time an observation survives the filter — so nothing changes there.
.PP
Group labels were truncated at a NUL and lost their UTF\-8 flag
.IX Subsection "Group labels were truncated at a NUL and lost their UTF-8 flag"
.PP
The \f(CW\*(C`x\*(C'\fR/\f(CW\*(C`g\*(C'\fR path read the label with \f(CW\*(C`SvPV_nolen\*(C'\fR and then took \f(CW\*(C`strlen\*(C'\fR of it.
Perl strings are counted, not NUL\-terminated, so \f(CW"a\e0X"\fR and \f(CW"a\e0Y"\fR
collapsed into one group: \f(CW\*(C`kruskal_test([1..6], ["a\e0X","a\e0X","a\e0Y","a\e0Y","b","b"])\*(C'\fR
came back as two groups with \f(CW\*(C`df = 1, H = 2.4\*(C'\fR instead of three with
\&\f(CW\*(C`df = 2, H = 4.571428571428573\*(C'\fR. Both paths also copied the label\*(Aqs bytes while
dropping perl\*(Aqs UTF\-8 flag when storing it into \f(CW\*(C`group.stats\*(C'\fR, so a label
outside latin\-1 came back as mojibake and the two input paths disagreed with
each other about labels inside it. The length now travels with the string and
carries the flag in its sign, which is \f(CW\*(C`hv_store\*(C'\fR\*(Aqs own convention, so a label
comes back \f(CW\*(C`eq\*(C'\fR to what went in. Dropping the \f(CW\*(C`strlen\*(C'\fR also drops a pass over
every label.
.PP
A trailing named argument read past the argument stack
.IX Subsection "A trailing named argument read past the argument stack"
.PP
The named\-argument loop took \f(CW\*(C`ST(arg_idx + 1)\*(C'\fR without checking that there was
one, so an odd argument list read one slot past the top of the stack — and what
it found there changed which branch ran: \f(CW\*(C`kruskal_test(\e%h, \*(Aqx\*(Aq)\*(C'\fR came back
complaining that \f(CW\*(Aqh\*(Aq\fR cannot be mixed with \f(CW\*(Aqx\*(Aq\fR/\f(CW\*(Aqg\*(Aq\fR, because \f(CW\*(C`x_sv\*(C'\fR had been
assigned whatever was past the end. \f(CW\*(C`binom_test\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`chisq_test\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`fisher_test\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CW\*(C`wilcox_test\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`var_test\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`prcomp\*(C'\fR all guard this; \f(CW\*(C`kruskal_test\*(C'\fR was the
one that did not. It now croaks \f(CW\*(C`odd number of named arguments\*(C'\fR.
.PP
An infinite chi\-squared statistic gave no p\-value
.IX Subsection "An infinite chi-squared statistic gave no p-value"
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`get_p_value\*(C'\fR short\-circuits a statistic at or below zero and otherwise goes to
\&\f(CW\*(C`igamc\*(C'\fR. \f(CW\*(C`+Inf\*(C'\fR is neither, so it reached the continued fraction, where the
first \f(CW\*(C`1/d\*(C'\fR is \f(CW\*(C`1/Inf = 0\*(C'\fR and then \f(CW\*(C`del = 0 * Inf\*(C'\fR is \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR — an overwhelmingly
significant result reported as no result at all. R\*(Aqs
\&\f(CW\*(C`pchisq(Inf, df, lower.tail = FALSE)\*(C'\fR is \f(CW0\fR, and so is this now. \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR in gives
\&\f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR out, as R does, rather than running the continued fraction to its full
10,000\-iteration safety bound first.
.PP
This is reachable from \f(CW\*(C`kruskal_test\*(C'\fR. When a sample has no variation at all
the tie correction is \f(CW\*(C`(n^3 \- n)/(n^3 \- n)\*(C'\fR, and once \f(CW\*(C`n^3\*(C'\fR is past \f(CW\*(C`2^53\*(C'\fR the
subtraction of \f(CW\*(C`n\*(C'\fR is lost from one side or the other, so an inexact zero is
divided by an exact zero. R has the same problem and returns \f(CW\*(C`+Inf\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`\-Inf\*(C'\fR or
\&\f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR depending on which way \f(CW\*(C`n\*(C'\fR rounded: \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR at \f(CW\*(C`n = 250000\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`\-Inf\*(C'\fR at
\&\f(CW300000\fR, \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR at \f(CW400000\fR, \f(CW\*(C`+Inf\*(C'\fR at \f(CW500000\fR, \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR at \f(CW750000\fR, \f(CW\*(C`+Inf\*(C'\fR at
\&\f(CW1000000\fR, \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR at \f(CW1500000\fR and \f(CW\*(C`+Inf\*(C'\fR at \f(CW2000000\fR. \f(CW\*(C`kruskal_test\*(C'\fR now agrees
with it on all eight. Below that the correction is exact and both give \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR,
which the corpus pins. \f(CW\*(C`get_p_value\*(C'\fR is shared, so \f(CW\*(C`chisq_test\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`prop_test\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CW\*(C`mcnemar_test\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`friedman_test\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`cmh_test\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`logrank_test\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`coxph\*(C'\fR get the
same fix.
.PP
Three times less memory, twice the speed
.IX Subsection "Three times less memory, twice the speed"
.PP
The ranking no longer goes through \f(CW\*(C`RankInfo\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`rank_and_count_ties\*(C'\fR.
\&\f(CW\*(C`kruskal_test\*(C'\fR wants per\-group rank sums, not the ranks themselves, so it sorts
a 16\-byte \f(CW\*(C`(value, group)\*(C'\fR pair and adds each tie block\*(Aqs averaged rank straight
into the group sums, instead of storing an \f(CW\*(C`NV\*(C'\fR rank per observation for a
second pass to read.
.PP
It also no longer calls \f(CW\*(C`qsort\*(C'\fR. glibc\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`qsort\*(C'\fR is a mergesort that allocates a
scratch buffer the size of the whole array — measured on glibc 2.39 as a
\&\f(CW\*(C`VmHWM\*(C'\fR of 116 MB going to 230 MB across one sort of a 114 MB array — which was
half of the function\*(Aqs peak memory, and its comparison goes through a function
pointer that cannot be inlined. In its place is a median\-of\-three introsort that
recurses on the smaller partition and loops on the larger, so the stack stays
\&\f(CW\*(C`O(log n)\*(C'\fR, with a heapsort fallback past a depth of \f(CW\*(C`2*floor(log2(n))\*(C'\fR so an
adversarial input cannot drive it to \f(CWO(n^2)\fR, and insertion sort for short
runs. Sorting the same five\-million\-element array takes 0.375s against \f(CW\*(C`qsort\*(C'\fR\*(Aqs
1.01s and allocates nothing. The third change is the group\-label array on the
\&\f(CW\*(C`x\*(C'\fR/\f(CW\*(C`g\*(C'\fR path, which was sized at one pointer per \fIobservation\fR to hold one per
\&\fIgroup\fR — 40 MB at \f(CW\*(C`n = 5e6\*(C'\fR to hold three pointers — and now grows on demand.
.PP
At \f(CW\*(C`n = 5,000,000\*(C'\fR over three groups, measured as \f(CW\*(C`VmHWM\*(C'\fR either side of the
call:
.PP
Sorted, reversed, all\-equal, organ\-pipe and median\-of\-three\-killer inputs all
stay under 0.32s at \f(CW\*(C`n = 2e6\*(C'\fR, which is what the depth limit is there for. The
sort is checked against an independent pure\-Perl implementation of the whole
test over 748 structured cases — those shapes at every n either side of the
insertion\-sort threshold — and 49,712 random ones.
.PP
The same input now gives the same answer
.IX Subsection "The same input now gives the same answer"
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`H\*(C'\fR moved by up to \f(CW1.2e\-14\fR between runs on identical data. Nothing was random:
the sum of \f(CW\*(C`R_i^2 / n_i\*(C'\fR walked the groups by group id, and on the
hash\-of\-arrays path an id is minted in \f(CW\*(C`hv_iternext\*(C'\fR order, which is perl\*(Aqs
per\-process hash order. Equal values were also left in whatever relative order
the sort happened to leave them, which came from the same place.
.PP
The sort now orders by value and then by group, which makes it a total order,
and the \f(CW\*(C`k\*(C'\fR terms of the sum are ordered before they are added — smallest first,
which is the better\-conditioned direction as well as a canonical one. \f(CW\*(C`k\*(C'\fR is the
number of groups, not the number of observations, so it costs nothing next to
the ranking. \f(CW\*(C`H\*(C'\fR is now bit\-identical to R on all 37 corpus cases in all four
call forms, and stays so across 60 runs under \f(CW\*(C`PERL_PERTURB_KEYS=1\*(C'\fR.
.PP
\fIDocumentation\fR
.IX Subsection "Documentation"
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`kruskal_test\*(C'\fR gains two sections: what happens to non\-numeric, undefined,
\&\f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR and infinite elements and to a group left with no data, and what the
returned fields are — \f(CW\*(C`statistic\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`parameter\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`method\*(C'\fR and the p\-value under
both \f(CW\*(C`p.value\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`p.value\*(C'\fR from R\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`htest\*(C'\fR, plus the \f(CW\*(C`size\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`mean\*(C'\fR
sub\-hashes of \f(CW\*(C`group.stats\*(C'\fR, which are computed over the same observations the
statistic used.
.SS "0.3 2026\-08\-16 CDT"
.IX Subsection "0.3 2026-08-16 CDT"
\fIshapiro_test\fR
.IX Subsection "shapiro_test"
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`shapiro_test\*(C'\fR rebuilt against R 4.6.1\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`src/library/stats/src/swilk.c\*(C'\fR — AS R94,
Royston (1995) — driven by R\*(Aqs and SciPy\*(Aqs own test suites rather than by cases
invented here. Four bugs are fixed, one of them a case R\*(Aqs regression suite
tests for by name, and the statistic is now more accurate than R\*(Aqs own on a
sample whose values dwarf its spread.
.PP
Everything below is checked in the new \f(CW\*(C`t/shapiro_test.R.scipy.t\*(C'\fR (146 tests),
whose expected values are frozen literals with their provenance recorded in the
file header; it needs no R and no Python to run. The generators that produced
them, \f(CW\*(C`t/shapiro_test.R.scipy.R\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`t/shapiro_test.R.scipy.py\*(C'\fR, are committed
beside it. The full suite is 124 files and 25,081 tests, and
\&\f(CW\*(C`./test.all.perls.pl\*(C'\fR passes on all five local perls — \f(CW5.10.1\fR, \f(CW5.12.5\fR
(long double), \f(CW5.42.3\fR, \f(CW5.44.0\fR and \f(CW\*(C`5.44.0\-quadmath\*(C'\fR — with no warnings on
any of them.
.PP
The p\-value could come back negative
.IX Subsection "The p-value could come back negative"
.PP
R\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`tests/reg\-tests\-1b.R\*(C'\fR contains exactly one \f(CW\*(C`shapiro.test\*(C'\fR assertion, and it
is this:
.PP
.Vb 1
\& stopifnot(shapiro.test(c(0,0,1))$p.value >= 0)
.Ve
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`shapiro_test([0,0,1])\*(C'\fR returned \f(CW\-4.6648135328131477e\-15\fR. At \f(CW\*(C`n = 3\*(C'\fR the
p\-value is \f(CW\*(C`6/pi * (asin(sqrt(W)) \- asin(sqrt(3/4)))\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`W\*(C'\fR has an exact floor
of \f(CW\*(C`3/4\*(C'\fR that \f(CW\*(C`c(0,0,1)\*(C'\fR sits on, so the subtraction lands on zero from
whichever side the constants round to; R clamps the result at 0 and this module
did not. It is the same defect SciPy fixed as gh\-18322. The clamp is in, and
\&\f(CW\*(C`asin(sqrt(3/4)) = pi/3\*(C'\fR is now carried to NV width rather than R\*(Aqs 15 digits,
so the same case comes out at \f(CW+4.2e\-16\fR before clamping instead of below zero.
.PP
W and the p\-value were only good to nine digits
.IX Subsection "W and the p-value were only good to nine digits"
.PP
The expected normal order statistics that AS R94 weights the sample with came
from \f(CWinverse_normal_cdf()\fR, which is Moro\*(Aqs approximation and good to about
\&\f(CW1e\-9\fR. They go straight into \f(CW\*(C`W\*(C'\fR, so nine digits there is nine digits in the
answer — where R reports sixteen. Against the values SciPy pins in
\&\f(CW\*(C`TestShapiro\*(C'\fR, every one of them annotated upstream as \fI"reference values
generated using R shapiro.test"\fR:
.PP
and the p\-values with them:
.PP
Moro\*(Aqs value is still the starting point, but Newton against the \f(CW\*(C`erfc\*(C'\fR\-based
normal CDF finishes it. The loop stops as soon as another pass could not move
the answer, so a \f(CW\*(C`double\*(C'\fR build pays for one refinement and only the wider NVs
pay for a second. Across a 180\-sample sweep over normal, uniform, exponential,
log\-normal, Cauchy, tied, tiny\-scale and grid data at every n from 3 to 5000,
the worst remaining disagreement with R is \f(CW2.0e\-15\fR in \f(CW\*(C`W\*(C'\fR and \f(CW2.6e\-12\fR in the
p\-value — the latter is not sloppier arithmetic but the same last bit amplified,
since the p\-value is a function of \f(CW\*(C`log(1 \- W)\*(C'\fR over a sigma of about \f(CW0.6\fR.
.PP
1 \- W was formed by subtracting from 1
.IX Subsection "1 - W was formed by subtracting from 1"
.PP
The p\-value depends on \f(CW\*(C`log(1 \- W)\*(C'\fR, and \f(CW\*(C`W\*(C'\fR runs to within \f(CW1e\-5\fR of 1 on a
large normal sample, so computing \f(CW\*(C`W = b^2/ssq\*(C'\fR and then \f(CW\*(C`1 \- W\*(C'\fR throws away
exactly the digits the p\-value is made of. R does not do this — its \f(CW\*(C`swilk.c\*(C'\fR
forms \f(CW\*(C`w1 = (ssassx \- sax) * (ssassx + sax) / (ssa * ssx)\*(C'\fR directly and says so
in a comment — and now neither does this module.
.PP
More accurate than R when the values dwarf their own spread
.IX Subsection "More accurate than R when the values dwarf their own spread"
.PP
R\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`swilk.c\*(C'\fR divides the sample by its range but never centres it, so \f(CW\*(C`1e9 +
noise\*(C'\fR loses most of its significant digits before \f(CW\*(C`W\*(C'\fR is ever formed. SciPy
filed the same complaint from the other end as gh\-14462 and works around it by
subtracting the median; this module now does that too, which costs one
subtraction per value.
.PP
Measured against a 60\-digit \f(CW\*(C`mpmath\*(C'\fR evaluation of AS R94 on the identical
doubles, over \f(CW\*(C`1e6 + noise\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`1e9 + noise\*(C'\fR at every n from 3 to 5000:
.PP
On well\-conditioned samples the two still agree to the last few ulp, so this is
a divergence only where R has already lost the digits. \f(CW\*(C`t/shapiro_test.R.scipy.t\*(C'\fR
asserts the invariance rather than R\*(Aqs number there, and records why at that
section.
.PP
Faster as well
.IX Subsection "Faster as well"
.PP
The sort now goes through the module\*(Aqs own introsort rather than \f(CWqsort()\fR,
whose comparator the compiler cannot inline; the order statistics are generated
for half the sample and mirrored, since the weights are antisymmetric; and ten
\&\f(CWpow()\fR calls became Horner evaluations. \f(CWpow()\fR is a \f(CW\*(C`_\|_float128\*(C'\fR call on a
quadmath perl.
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`n = 100\*(C'\fR is the one size that got slower: at that length the accurate
quantiles are most of the work and there is not enough sorting to pay for them.
That trade was taken deliberately.
.PP
One documented value was wrong
.IX Subsection "One documented value was wrong"
.PP
The hash printed under \f(CW\*(C`shapiro_test\*(C'\fR in this README, in \f(CW\*(C`read.me.pod\*(C'\fR and in
the module\*(Aqs own POD showed \f(CW\*(C`statistic 0.960870680168535\*(C'\fR and \f(CWp.value
0.589650577093106\fR — the pre\-fix numbers, and for \f(CW\*(C`[1..19]\*(C'\fR while the example
above them calls \f(CWshapiro_test([1..5])\fR. It now shows what \f(CW\*(C`[1..5]\*(C'\fR actually
returns, \f(CW0.986762155447719\fR and \f(CW0.96717393596804\fR, which is R\*(Aqs
\&\f(CW\*(C`shapiro.test(1:5)\*(C'\fR to the last digit R prints.
.PP
\fIquantile\fR
.IX Subsection "quantile"
.PP
Interpolation ran between order statistics that were equal
.IX Subsection "Interpolation ran between order statistics that were equal"
.PP
R\*(Aqs type 7 interpolates only when the index falls strictly between two order
statistics \fIthat differ\fR — \f(CW\*(C`index > lo & x[hi] != qs\*(C'\fR in \f(CW\*(C`quantile.default\*(C'\fR.
This module always evaluated \f(CW\*(C`(1 \- g) * x[j] + g * x[j+1]\*(C'\fR, which does not
return \f(CW\*(C`v\*(C'\fR when both sides are \f(CW\*(C`v\*(C'\fR. On a two\-valued sample at \f(CW\*(C`n = 999\*(C'\fR it
reported \f(CW0.99999999999994\fR for \f(CW1\fR, and on the 602 identical values R\*(Aqs
PR#16672 was filed about it failed to return that value at every prob — which
is the monotonicity failure the PR is about. Both now match R exactly.
.PP
Probabilities a hair outside [0, 1] were refused
.IX Subsection "Probabilities a hair outside [0, 1] were refused"
.PP
A probability arrived at by arithmetic rather than written down can land just
outside the interval. R allows \f(CW\*(C`100 * .Machine$double.eps\*(C'\fR of overshoot and
clamps to the endpoint — its PR#17891, \f(CW\*(C`quantile(0:1, 1+1e\-14) == 1\*(C'\fR — where
this module raised an error. It now clamps within the same allowance and still
errors on anything further out. R\*(Aqs constant is used rather than \f(CW\*(C`NV_EPSILON\*(C'\fR
on purpose: it is part of what the function \fIaccepts\fR, so a long\-double or
\&\f(CW\*(C`_\|_float128\*(C'\fR build must not reject a \f(CW\*(C`probs\*(C'\fR vector R takes.
.PP
Faster
.IX Subsection "Faster"
.PP
The sort was \f(CWqsort()\fR with a function\-pointer comparator; it is now the same
introsort \f(CW\*(C`shapiro_test\*(C'\fR uses. Ordering 5000 NVs costs about 61,000
comparisons, and paying for an indirect call on every one of them is most of
what a sort of that size costs.
.PP
Both fixes and the sort are covered by the new \f(CW\*(C`t/quantile.R.t\*(C'\fR (197 tests, or
205 under \f(CW\*(C`EXTENDED_TESTING\*(C'\fR), built on the two assertions R\*(Aqs own suite makes
about \f(CW\*(C`quantile\*(C'\fR — that \f(CW\*(C`quantile(x, ((1:n)\-1)/(n\-1))\*(C'\fR recovers \f(CWsort(x)\fR, and
that it equals the type\-7 interpolation computed by hand off the sorted sample
— run over seven input shapes chosen to break a quicksort (sorted, reversed,
organ pipe, two\-valued, tie ladder, sawtooth) at every n either side of the
insertion\-sort threshold and the recursion depth limit, plus PR#16672 and
PR#17891 verbatim and 79 frozen R value tables. Its generator,
\&\f(CW\*(C`t/quantile.R.R\*(C'\fR, is committed beside it.
.PP
\fIDocumentation\fR
.IX Subsection "Documentation"
.PP
Illustrations for three more functions, drawn by \f(CW\*(C`t.test.plots.pl\*(C'\fR and
\&\f(CW\*(C`skew.kurtosis.plots.pl\*(C'\fR, both committed:
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fR\f(CB\*(C`t_test\*(C'\fR\fB\fR gains six: what the estimate, the standard error and the null
distribution are and which area of it the p\-value is; how \f(CW\*(C`conf.int\*(C'\fR is the
estimate plus or minus a t quantile and how \f(CW\*(C`conf.level\*(C'\fR sets that quantile;
the three \f(CW\*(C`alternative\*(C'\fRs side by side with the region each counts and the
interval that goes with it; \f(CW\*(C`p.value\*(C'\fR as a function of \f(CW\*(C`mu\*(C'\fR, crossing
\&\f(CW\*(C`1 \- conf.level\*(C'\fR exactly at the two bounds of \f(CW\*(C`conf.int\*(C'\fR; paired,
\&\f(CW\*(C`var_equal\*(C'\fR and Welch on the same data, with the Welch degrees of freedom as
the two spreads separate; and two distributions separating with the interval
retreating from \f(CW\*(C`mu\*(C'\fR as the p\-value falls.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fR\f(CB\*(C`skew\*(C'\fR\fB\fR gains a left\-tailed, a symmetric and a right\-tailed sample against
the same \f(CW\*(C`N(0, 1)\*(C'\fR curve, with the mean and median of each, which is what the
sign of the statistic is reporting.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fR\f(CB\*(C`kurtosis\*(C'\fR\fB\fR gains a flat\-shouldered, a normal and a heavy\-tailed sample
with the tails behind each drawn out, since it is the tails and not the peak
that the statistic is measuring.
.SS "0.298 2026\-08\-12 CDT"
.IX Subsection "0.298 2026-08-12 CDT"
\fIwilcox_test\fR
.IX Subsection "wilcox_test"
.PP
A rewrite of \f(CW\*(C`wilcox_test\*(C'\fR against R 4.6.1, driven by R\*(Aqs and SciPy\*(Aqs own test
suites rather than by cases invented here. It brings the function up to the
exact conditional inference R gained in 4.6.0, fixes six bugs — two of which
returned confidently wrong p\-values on the \fIdefault\fR code path — and adds the
Hodges\-Lehmann estimate and confidence interval, \f(CW\*(C`digits.rank\*(C'\fR, and the
Edgeworth series.
.PP
Everything below is checked in the new \f(CW\*(C`t/wilcox_test.R.scipy.t\*(C'\fR (3,242 tests),
whose expected values are frozen literals with their provenance recorded in the
file header; it needs no R and no Python to run. The full suite is 120 files and
23,149 tests, and \f(CW\*(C`./test.all.perls.pl\*(C'\fR passes on all five local perls —
\&\f(CW5.10.1\fR, \f(CW5.12.5\fR (long double), \f(CW5.42.3\fR, \f(CW5.44.0\fR and \f(CW\*(C`5.44.0\-quadmath\*(C'\fR —
with no warnings on any of them.
.PP
Exact p\-values are now computed when there are ties
.IX Subsection "Exact p-values are now computed when there are ties"
.PP
R 4.6.0 added exact (conditional) inference in the presence of ties, via Torsten
Hothorn\*(Aqs implementation of the Streitberg\-Röhmel shift algorithm; R\*(Aqs
\&\f(CW\*(C`doc/NEWS.Rd\*(C'\fR announces it and \f(CW\*(C`tests/reg\-tests\-1d.R\*(C'\fR records the consequence at
its degenerate one\-sample cases: \fI"For R >= 4.6.0 warnings for exact with ties
are gone."\fR Before that, ties ruled out an exact p\-value and both R and this
module fell back to the normal approximation with a warning.
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`wilcox_test\*(C'\fR now does what R does. When ties are present the null distribution
is the conditional one given the observed ranks, and the same holds for zero
differences in the signed\-rank test. The warnings are gone with them.
.PP
This changes published answers on tied data, including R\*(Aqs own documented
examples:
.PP
Two further consequences are worth knowing about. \fBV\fR itself changes when zero
differences are present, because the exact test ranks \f(CW\*(C`|x \- mu|\*(C'\fR over every
observation and only afterwards drops the ranks belonging to the zeroes, where
the approximation drops the zeroes first and ranks what is left:
\&\f(CW\*(C`wilcox_test([\-1, 0, 1])\*(C'\fR gives \f(CW\*(C`V = 2.5\*(C'\fR exactly and \f(CW\*(C`V = 1.5\*(C'\fR with
\&\f(CW\*(C`exact => 0\*(C'\fR. R\*(Aqs two branches differ in exactly the same way. And degenerate
inputs that used to be fatal now return a result, as they must for
\&\f(CW\*(C`tests/reg\-tests\-1d.R\*(C'\fR line 332 to pass: \f(CWwilcox_test([0])\fR gives \f(CW\*(C`V = 0\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CW\*(C`p = 1\*(C'\fR, and so does \f(CW\*(C`wilcox_test([0,0,0,0,0])\*(C'\fR, which SciPy pins as
\&\f(CW\*(C`test_all_zeros_exact\*(C'\fR.
.PP
If you need the old numbers, \f(CW\*(C`exact => 0\*(C'\fR still asks for the approximation and
is unchanged.
.PP
The exact upper tail was returning zero, on the default path
.IX Subsection "The exact upper tail was returning zero, on the default path"
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`p_greater\*(C'\fR was computed as \f(CW\*(C`1 \- CDF(q \- 1)\*(C'\fR. That subtraction cancels away every
significant digit once the true p falls below \f(CW\*(C`NV_EPSILON\*(C'\fR, and then returns a
flat \f(CW0\fR. It did not take a contrived input to reach: two perfectly separated
samples of 30 apiece are inside the automatic exact branch, no \f(CW\*(C`exact => 1\*(C'\fR
required.
.PP
Both tails are now summed directly. That alone is not enough for the rank\-sum
table, whose Gaussian\-binomial recurrence is built with subtractions, so far up
the support a count of \f(CW1\fR is the difference of numbers around \f(CW\*(C`C(m+n, n)\*(C'\fR and
has already been rounded into noise. The table is folded about its centre before
summing, so only well\-conditioned entries are ever touched — the same thing R\*(Aqs
\&\f(CWpwilcox()\fR does when it folds \f(CW\*(C`q\*(C'\fR about \f(CW\*(C`m*n/2\*(C'\fR and flips \f(CW\*(C`lower_tail\*(C'\fR.
.PP
The signed\-rank tail was accurate to \f(CW\*(C`n = 49\*(C'\fR by luck (\f(CW\*(C`1 \- 2^\-49\*(C'\fR is exactly
representable) and reached \f(CW0\fR from about \f(CW\*(C`n = 53\*(C'\fR; forcing
\&\f(CW\*(C`exact => 1\*(C'\fR on \f(CW\*(C`n = 120\*(C'\fR returned \f(CW0\fR where R gives \f(CW1.5046327690525337e\-36\fR,
and now returns it too.
.PP
\f(CW\*(C`int m * n\*(C'\fR overflowed, and said the samples were identical
.IX Subsection "int m * n overflowed, and said the samples were identical"
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`exact_pwilcox\*(C'\fR took \f(CW\*(C`int m, int n\*(C'\fR and computed \f(CW\*(C`int max_u = m * n\*(C'\fR. For two
separated samples of 50,000 that wraps negative, every statistic looks out of
range, and the function returns \f(CW1.0\fR:
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`perl
wilcox_test([1 .. 50000], [50001 .. 100000], exact => 1);   # p = 1\*(C'\fR
.PP
Signed overflow is also undefined behaviour, so a different optimiser was
entitled to do something else entirely. Sizes and indices in the exact
distributions are \f(CW\*(C`size_t\*(C'\fR now, the multiplications are checked for wrap before
they happen, and a table that would need more than 16 million cells is refused
outright with a message naming \f(CW\*(C`exact => 0\*(C'\fR rather than attempted.
.PP
NaN was ranked instead of dropped
.IX Subsection "NaN was ranked instead of dropped"
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR is \f(CW\*(C`NA\*(C'\fR to R, and R drops it. \f(CW\*(C`looks_like_number\*(C'\fR accepts it, \f(CW\*(C`d == 0.0\*(C'\fR
is false for it, so it went into the rank buffer — and \f(CW\*(C`cmp_nv3\*(C'\fR returns \f(CW0\fR for
every comparison involving it, which leaves \f(CW\*(C`qsort\*(C'\fR without the strict weak
ordering the C standard entitles it to.
.PP
The visible symptom is R\*(Aqs own regression case, \f(CW\*(C`tests/reg\-tests\-1d.R\*(C'\fR line
3546, which asserts that a paired test is unaffected by pairs whose difference
is \f(CW\*(C`Inf \- Inf\*(C'\fR:
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR — in either sample, and however it arises — is now dropped with the other
missing values. \f(CW\*(C`±Inf\*(C'\fR is not missing and is kept, since a rank test has no
trouble with it; SciPy\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`test_gh_11355b\*(C'\fR pins five cases of that and they all
agree.
.PP
An empty \f(CW\*(C`y\*(C'\fR ran a different test
.IX Subsection "An empty y ran a different test"
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`wilcox_test([1,2,3], [])\*(C'\fR fell through to the one\-sample branch and returned a
signed\-rank result, silently answering a question nobody asked. It croaks now,
with R\*(Aqs message.
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`mu\*(C'\fR was likewise unvalidated: \f(CW\*(C`mu => Inf\*(C'\fR or \f(CW\*(C`mu => NaN\*(C'\fR turned every
difference into a non\-number and produced a confident answer from the wreckage.
Both croak now, as they do in R.
.PP
A dying \f(CW$SIG{_\|_WARN_\|_}\fR handler leaked the rank buffer
.IX Subsection "A dying $SIG{__WARN__} handler leaked the rank buffer"
.PP
The warnings in \f(CW\*(C`wilcox_test\*(C'\fR were emitted while the \f(CW\*(C`RankInfo\*(C'\fR and difference
buffers were held as raw pointers. A \f(CW\*(C`_\|_WARN_\|_\*(C'\fR handler that dies — or \f(CW\*(C`warnings
FATAL\*(C'\fR at the call site — longjmps straight past the \f(CW\*(C`Safefree\*(C'\fR. Under valgrind,
500 iterations of the ties path with such a handler lost 95,616 bytes in 498
blocks. Every allocation now goes through \f(CW\*(C`Newx\*(C'\fR plus \f(CW\*(C`SAVEFREEPV\*(C'\fR, the idiom
\&\f(CW\*(C`chisq_test\*(C'\fR in the same file already used, so it is released by the save stack
however the call unwinds. The same 500 iterations now report \f(CW\*(C`definitely lost: 0
bytes\*(C'\fR, as does a sweep over every croak path and every branch of the function.
.PP
New: \f(CW\*(C`conf.int\*(C'\fR, and a Hodges\-Lehmann estimate
.IX Subsection "New: conf.int, and a Hodges-Lehmann estimate"
.PP
R has returned a distribution\-free confidence interval and a point estimate
since PR#1150 in 2001, and \f(CW\*(C`tests/reg\-tests\-1a.R\*(C'\fR has guarded them ever since
with Hollander & Wolfe\*(Aqs published numbers. \f(CW\*(C`wilcox_test\*(C'\fR now computes both, by
all four of R\*(Aqs routes — the exact interval from the order statistics of the
Walsh averages or the pairwise differences, the exact interval conditional on
the observed ranks when there are ties, and the asymptotic interval from a root
search:
.PP
\&\`\`\`perl
my \f(CW$r\fR = wilcox_test(\e@y, \e@x, paired => 1, conf_int => 1);
.ie n .SH "$r\->{estimate}   == \-0.46"
.el .SH "\f(CW$r\fP\->{estimate}   == \-0.46"
.IX Header "$r->{estimate} == -0.46"
.ie n .SH "$r\->{conf.int}   == [\-0.786, \-0.010]"
.el .SH "\f(CW$r\fP\->{conf.int}   == [\-0.786, \-0.010]"
.IX Header "$r->{conf.int} == [-0.786, -0.010]"
.ie n .SH "$r\->{conf.level} == 0.9609375"
.el .SH "\f(CW$r\fP\->{conf.level} == 0.9609375"
.IX Header "$r->{conf.level} == 0.9609375"
\&\`\`\`
.PP
Those are Hollander & Wolfe (1999) 2nd ed., pp. 40 and 53, to the digit. So are
the two\-sample values from pp. 111 and 126: estimate \f(CW\-0.305\fR, interval
\&\f(CW\*(C`(\-0.76, 0.15)\*(C'\fR.
.PP
The level a rank test can actually deliver is a step function of the data, so
\&\f(CW\*(C`conf.level\*(C'\fR reports what was achieved rather than echoing what was asked for —
\&\f(CW0.9609375\fR above, not \f(CW0.95\fR. \f(CW\*(C`conf.level\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`tol.root\*(C'\fR and R\*(Aqs alpha\-doubling
search for a level the data can support (with its \fIrequested conf.level not
achievable\fR warning) all behave as R\*(Aqs do.
.PP
New: \f(CW\*(C`digits.rank\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`edgeworth\*(C'\fR, and more of R\*(Aqs result fields
.IX Subsection "New: digits.rank, edgeworth, and more of R's result fields"
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`digits.rank\*(C'\fR rounds each value to a given number of significant digits before
ranking, so that ties are decided on the rounded values. R\*(Aqs man page recommends
it because tie detection is an exact \f(CW\*(C`==\*(C'\fR on floating point, and its own worked
example shows \f(CW\*(C`(4:2)/10\*(C'\fR against \f(CW\*(C`(3:1)/10\*(C'\fR — three differences that ought to be
\&\f(CW0.1\fR and are three different doubles. Ported from R\*(Aqs \f(CWfprec()\fR, half\-to\-even
rounding included.
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`edgeworth => 1, 2, 3\*(C'\fR adds up to three Edgeworth correction terms to the normal
approximation, the refinement R 4.6.0 reaches through its integer \f(CW\*(C`correct\*(C'\fR. It
is ignored on the exact path, and — as in R — ignored when there are ties, or
when the signed\-rank test dropped a zero, because the series is derived for
untied ranks.
.PP
The result hash gains \f(CW\*(C`statistic.name\*(C'\fR (\f(CW"W"\fR or \f(CW"V"\fR, as R prints), plus
\&\f(CW\*(C`null.value\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`null.value.name\*(C'\fR, and \f(CW\*(C`estimate\*(C'\fR / \f(CW\*(C`conf.int\*(C'\fR / \f(CW\*(C`conf.level\*(C'\fR
when an interval was asked for.
.PP
Three deliberate differences from R
.IX Subsection "Three deliberate differences from R"
.PP
Each is asserted in the test file, so that changing one later is a choice rather
than a drift.
.ie n .IP "1. \fR\fB""correct""\fR\fB is a boolean here.\fR R 4.6.0 turned its ""correct"" into an integer ""0:3"", in which numeric 0 still applies the continuity correction and only ""FALSE"" removes it — so in R, ""correct = 0"" and ""correct = FALSE"" are different tests. Keeping that would mean ""correct => 0"" no longer meaning ""off"", which is what it means for every other flag in this module. ""correct"" stays a boolean, and R\*(Aqs ""correct = k"" is ""correct => 1, edgeworth => k""." 4
.el .IP "1. \fR\f(CBcorrect\fR\fB is a boolean here.\fR R 4.6.0 turned its \f(CWcorrect\fR into an integer \f(CW0:3\fR, in which numeric \f(CW0\fR still applies the continuity correction and only \f(CWFALSE\fR removes it — so in R, \f(CWcorrect = 0\fR and \f(CWcorrect = FALSE\fR are different tests. Keeping that would mean \f(CWcorrect => 0\fR no longer meaning ""off"", which is what it means for every other flag in this module. \f(CWcorrect\fR stays a boolean, and R\*(Aqs \f(CWcorrect = k\fR is \f(CWcorrect => 1, edgeworth => k\fR." 4
.IX Item "1. correct is a boolean here. R 4.6.0 turned its correct into an integer 0:3, in which numeric 0 still applies the continuity correction and only FALSE removes it — so in R, correct = 0 and correct = FALSE are different tests. Keeping that would mean correct => 0 no longer meaning ""off"", which is what it means for every other flag in this module. correct stays a boolean, and R's correct = k is correct => 1, edgeworth => k."
.PD 0
.ie n .IP "2. \fBA zero variance is reported, not propagated.\fR With ""exact => 0"" and every observation tied there is nothing to divide by. R divides anyway and returns ""NaN""; this warns and returns ""p = 1"". The default path no longer reaches it at all, since the exact test handles all\-tied data." 4
.el .IP "2. \fBA zero variance is reported, not propagated.\fR With \f(CWexact => 0\fR and every observation tied there is nothing to divide by. R divides anyway and returns \f(CWNaN\fR; this warns and returns \f(CWp = 1\fR. The default path no longer reaches it at all, since the exact test handles all\-tied data." 4
.IX Item "2. A zero variance is reported, not propagated. With exact => 0 and every observation tied there is nothing to divide by. R divides anyway and returns NaN; this warns and returns p = 1. The default path no longer reaches it at all, since the exact test handles all-tied data."
.ie n .IP "3. \fBAn all\-tied interval does not raise.\fR R\*(Aqs one\-sample code warns and hands back a ""NaN"" interval at level 0; its two\-sample code warns and then dies inside ""uniroot"" with \fImissing value where TRUE/FALSE needed\fR. We give the one\-sample answer in both places." 4
.el .IP "3. \fBAn all\-tied interval does not raise.\fR R\*(Aqs one\-sample code warns and hands back a \f(CWNaN\fR interval at level \f(CW0\fR; its two\-sample code warns and then dies inside \f(CWuniroot\fR with \fImissing value where TRUE/FALSE needed\fR. We give the one\-sample answer in both places." 4
.IX Item "3. An all-tied interval does not raise. R's one-sample code warns and hands back a NaN interval at level 0; its two-sample code warns and then dies inside uniroot with missing value where TRUE/FALSE needed. We give the one-sample answer in both places."
.PD
.PP
There is one place where this module is simply more accurate than R. R\*(Aqs exact
p\-values on tied data come from a density it normalises entry by entry;
\&\f(CW\*(C`wilcox_test\*(C'\fR sums the integer permutation counts and divides once. For the
worst case in the corpus — an 11\-against\-12 tied rank sum whose p\-value is
exactly \f(CW\*(C`4/676039\*(C'\fR — this returns the correctly rounded double and R is
\&\f(CW1.2e\-11\fR high. Checked against exact rational arithmetic, and recorded in the
test file rather than papered over.
.PP
Testing
.IX Subsection "Testing"
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`t/wilcox_test.R.scipy.t\*(C'\fR takes its cases from the references\*(Aq own suites:
.IP \(bu 4
R\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`tests/reg\-tests\-1a.R\*(C'\fR (the PR#1150 Hollander & Wolfe intervals),
\&\f(CW\*(C`reg\-tests\-1b.R\*(C'\fR (the Wolfgang Huber \f(CW\*(C`wilcox.test(1, 2:60)\*(C'\fR case, and the
check that the asymptotic estimate does not move with \f(CW\*(C`alternative\*(C'\fR),
\&\f(CW\*(C`reg\-tests\-1d.R\*(C'\fR (the six degenerate one\-sample calls and the \f(CW\*(C`±Inf\*(C'\fR
identities), and the man\-page examples whose printed output is pinned in
\&\f(CW\*(C`tests/Examples/stats\-Ex.Rout.save\*(C'\fR.
.IP \(bu 4
SciPy 1.17.1\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`TestMannWhitneyU\*(C'\fR, whose header reads \fI"All magic numbers are
from R wilcox.test"\fR — \f(CW\*(C`cases_basic\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`cases_continuity\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`cases_9184\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CW\*(C`cases_2118\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`test_tie_correct\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`test_exact_U_equals_mean\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CW\*(C`test_gh_11355b\*(C'\fR and the 30\-against\-20 asymptotic cases — and
\&\f(CW\*(C`TestWilcoxon\*(C'\fR\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`test_accuracy_wilcoxon\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`test_wilcoxon_tie\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CW\*(C`test_onesided\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`test_exact_pval\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`test_exact_p_1\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`test_all_zeros_exact\*(C'\fR
and \f(CW\*(C`test_symmetry_gh19872_gh20752\*(C'\fR.
.IP \(bu 4
A 663\-case sweep generated by \f(CW\*(C`t/wilcox_test.R.scipy.R\*(C'\fR, committed next to the
test, crossing four data shapes against every alternative, \f(CW\*(C`exact\*(C'\fR state,
\&\f(CW\*(C`correct\*(C'\fR state, \f(CW\*(C`mu\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`conf.int\*(C'\fR setting.
.PP
Beyond the file, 960 further randomised calls were compared against R 4.6.1 and
agree everywhere except the three divergences above.
.PP
One lesson from getting that to pass on every NV width is worth recording: the
corpus data has to be \fBexactly representable\fR. Whether two values tie decides
which branch runs, and \f(CW\*(C`1.6 \- 2 \- 0.5\*(C'\fR does not land on the same value in a
\&\f(CW\*(C`double\*(C'\fR, an x87 \f(CW\*(C`long double\*(C'\fR and a \f(CW\*(C`_\|_float128\*(C'\fR. A corpus of one\-decimal
values passed on the default perl and failed on \f(CW\*(C`perl\-5.12.5\*(C'\fR and quadmath with
a \fIdifferent statistic\fR, not merely a different last digit. Every generated
value is now a whole number of quarters or of 1024ths. For the same reason the
asymptotic interval, which is only ever pinned down to \f(CW\*(C`tol.root\*(C'\fR, is generated
at \f(CW\*(C`tol.root = 1e\-12\*(C'\fR rather than freezing wherever Brent\*(Aqs method happened to
stop on one machine.
.PP
A compiler\-warning audit of \f(CW\*(C`LikeR.xs\*(C'\fR for \f(CW\*(C`\-Wint\-conversion\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`\-Wimplicit\-int\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CW\*(C`\-Wreturn\-mismatch\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`\-Wdeclaration\-missing\-parameter\-type\*(C'\fR, and a pass
tightening integer types that can only hold a count or a flag. No behaviour
changed: the full suite (116 files, 18,546 tests) passes, and every function
touched was diffed call\-for\-call against a build of the previous release, with
\&\f(CW\*(C`dnorm\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`pnorm\*(C'\fR, one\- and two\-sample \f(CW\*(C`ks_test\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`fisher_test\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`auc\*(C'\fR and the set
operations re\-checked against R 4.6.1 and found bit\-identical.
.PP
All four of those warnings were already clean, and stay clean on a \f(CW\*(C`double\*(C'\fR, a
\&\f(CW\*(C`long double\*(C'\fR and a \f(CW\*(C`_\|_float128\*(C'\fR build. Two of them cannot be tested with the
GCC most systems still default to: \f(CW\*(C`\-Wreturn\-mismatch\*(C'\fR and
\&\f(CW\*(C`\-Wdeclaration\-missing\-parameter\-type\*(C'\fR are GCC 14 additions — where they are
errors rather than warnings — and GCC 13 rejects both as unrecognized options,
so a check that appears to pass on 13 has really only skipped them.
.PP
\fIDead code removed\fR
.IX Subsection "Dead code removed"
.PP
Turning the audit up to \f(CW\*(C`\-Wextra\*(C'\fR found two branches that could never run, both
of them a test for negativity on a value whose type is unsigned:
.ie n .IP "1. ""r_pow_di"" takes ""unsigned int n"", so its ""if (n < 0) return 1.0 / r_pow_di(x, \-n);"" was unreachable — a leftover of R\*(Aqs ""R_pow_di"", which takes a signed exponent. All three callers (in ""K2x"", for the exact one\-sample Kolmogorov\-Smirnov distribution) pass a non\-negative exponent, so the unsigned parameter is the correct one and the reciprocal branch simply goes." 4
.el .IP "1. \f(CWr_pow_di\fR takes \f(CWunsigned int n\fR, so its \f(CWif (n < 0) return 1.0 / r_pow_di(x, \-n);\fR was unreachable — a leftover of R\*(Aqs \f(CWR_pow_di\fR, which takes a signed exponent. All three callers (in \f(CWK2x\fR, for the exact one\-sample Kolmogorov\-Smirnov distribution) pass a non\-negative exponent, so the unsigned parameter is the correct one and the reciprocal branch simply goes." 4
.IX Item "1. r_pow_di takes unsigned int n, so its if (n < 0) return 1.0 / r_pow_di(x, -n); was unreachable — a leftover of R's R_pow_di, which takes a signed exponent. All three callers (in K2x, for the exact one-sample Kolmogorov-Smirnov distribution) pass a non-negative exponent, so the unsigned parameter is the correct one and the reciprocal branch simply goes."
.PD 0
.ie n .IP "2. ""hoa2aoh"" casts ""HvUSEDKEYS"" to ""U32"" and then clamps with ""if (ncols < 0) ncols = 0;""." 4
.el .IP "2. \f(CWhoa2aoh\fR casts \f(CWHvUSEDKEYS\fR to \f(CWU32\fR and then clamps with \f(CWif (ncols < 0) ncols = 0;\fR." 4
.IX Item "2. hoa2aoh casts HvUSEDKEYS to U32 and then clamps with if (ncols < 0) ncols = 0;."
.PD
.PP
\fITypes narrowed to what they can actually hold\fR
.IX Subsection "Types narrowed to what they can actually hold"
.PP
Eighteen \f(CW\*(C`int\*(C'\fRs that only ever hold 0 or 1 became \f(CW\*(C`bool\*(C'\fR, a convention the file
already followed in some 219 other places; each was confirmed by reading every
call site rather than by name. The flag parameters of \f(CW\*(C`ft_pnhyper\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`K2l\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CW\*(C`c_dnorm\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`c_pnorm\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`c_pnorm_both\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`set_multiplicity\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`roc_split\*(C'\fR, the
\&\f(CW\*(C`is_cat\*(C'\fR field of \f(CW\*(C`AnFac\*(C'\fR, the \f(CW\*(C`lower_pos\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`frac_low\*(C'\fR locals of \f(CW\*(C`auc\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CW\*(C`auroc\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`roc\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`bedroc\*(C'\fR, and the return types of \f(CW\*(C`mg_key\*(C'\fR and
\&\f(CW\*(C`psmirnov_exact_test\*(C'\fR. Several of these were already being handed a \f(CW\*(C`bool\*(C'\fR by
their callers — \f(CW\*(C`dnorm\*(C'\fR\*(Aqs and \f(CW\*(C`pnorm\*(C'\fR\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`log\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`lower\*(C'\fR options, for instance —
so only the helper signatures were behind. \f(CW\*(C`c_pnorm_both\*(C'\fR\*(Aqs loop counter became
\&\f(CW\*(C`unsigned int\*(C'\fR.
.PP
Two that look like flags and are not: \f(CW\*(C`c_pnorm_both\*(C'\fR\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`i_tail\*(C'\fR is three\-valued,
and \f(CW\*(C`set_multiplicity\*(C'\fR\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`gimme\*(C'\fR carries a Perl \f(CW\*(C`G_*\*(C'\fR context value. Both stay
\&\f(CW\*(C`int\*(C'\fR.
.PP
Also six coefficient tables in \f(CW\*(C`c_pnorm_both\*(C'\fR written \f(CW\*(C`const static double\*(C'\fR,
which puts the storage class after the qualifier and draws
\&\f(CW\*(C`\-Wold\-style\-declaration\*(C'\fR; they are now \f(CW\*(C`static const double\*(C'\fR.
.PP
\fIrunif argument validation, and every warning names its function\fR
.IX Subsection "runif argument validation, and every warning names its function"
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`runif\*(C'\fR accepts its arguments either positionally or by name, and decided which
was which by asking whether the current argument was a string \fIand\fR whether
another argument followed it. A key at the end of the list therefore failed the
second half of that test and fell through to the positional branch, where it was
read as a number: \f(CW\*(C`runif(5, \*(Aqmin\*(Aq)\*(C'\fR took \f(CWSvNV("min")\fR, which is 0, silently set
\&\f(CW\*(C`min = 0\*(C'\fR, and returned five values. The only sign anything was wrong was perl\*(Aqs
own \f(CW\*(C`Argument "min" isn\*(Aqt numeric\*(C'\fR, which does not say which function provoked
it. \f(CW\*(C`runif(5, bogus => 1)\*(C'\fR went the same way, taking \f(CW\*(C`bogus\*(C'\fR as \f(CW\*(C`min\*(C'\fR and \f(CW1\fR as
\&\f(CW\*(C`max\*(C'\fR. Every sibling that parses named arguments — \f(CW\*(C`rbinom\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`binom_test\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CW\*(C`fisher_test\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`dnorm\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`pnorm\*(C'\fR — rejects both of those.
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`runif\*(C'\fR now does too. A string argument is treated as a key when it is not a
number, which is decidable from the key alone, so a dangling or misspelled key
is an error instead of a silent coercion; a numeric string is still positional,
so \f(CWrunif("9")\fR is unchanged. Named values are checked for numerichood before
use, which is what keeps perl\*(Aqs unattributed warning from being the diagnostic.
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`n\*(C'\fR is also range\-checked now. It was read straight through \f(CWSvUV()\fR, so
\&\f(CWrunif(\-1)\fR wrapped to 2**64\-1, \f(CWav_extend()\fR read that back as a negative
\&\f(CW\*(C`SSize_t\*(C'\fR, and perl died with \f(CW\*(C`panic: av_extend_guts() negative count (\-2)\*(C'\fR \-\-
which names neither the function nor the argument at fault. A negative or
over\-large \f(CW\*(C`n\*(C'\fR now croaks and says so. Non\-integer \f(CW\*(C`n\*(C'\fR still truncates toward
zero, as R\*(Aqs \f(CWrunif()\fR does, and \f(CWrunif(0)\fR still returns an empty list.
.PP
Separately, three warnings did not name the function emitting them, unlike every
other warning in the file: one in \f(CW\*(C`ks_test\*(C'\fR (the 1\-sided exact 1\-sample case
falling back to asymptotic) and two in \f(CW\*(C`wilcox_test\*(C'\fR\*(Aqs signed\-rank branch (exact
p\-value abandoned for ties, and for zeroes). All three now carry the prefix
their siblings already had. The one warning left deliberately bare is the
\&\f(CW\*(C`warn("%s", m)\*(C'\fR in the uninitialized\-value catcher, which re\-emits somebody
else\*(Aqs warning verbatim and must not add to it.
.PP
\fIArgument\-stack indices are now Stack_off_t\fR
.IX Subsection "Argument-stack indices are now Stack_off_t"
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`\-Wextra\*(C'\fR reported 58 \f(CW\*(C`\-Wsign\-compare\*(C'\fR warnings, and 33 of them were one idiom:
an index declared \f(CW\*(C`size_t\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`unsigned\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`unsigned int\*(C'\fR or \f(CW\*(C`unsigned short int\*(C'\fR
and then compared against \f(CW\*(C`items\*(C'\fR. \f(CW\*(C`items\*(C'\fR is neither of those — XSUB.h\*(Aqs
\&\f(CW\*(C`dITEMS\*(C'\fR declares it \f(CW\*(C`Stack_off_t items = (Stack_off_t)(SP \- MARK)\*(C'\fR, a \fIsigned\fR
type, because it is a stack\-pointer difference. Every one of those comparisons
was converting the signed side to unsigned.
.PP
The indices are now \f(CW\*(C`Stack_off_t\*(C'\fR themselves, which is the type they are
compared against: 25 declarations across 23 functions — \f(CW\*(C`binom_test\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CW\*(C`ks_test\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`wilcox_test\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`write_table\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`max\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`runif\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`quantile\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`mean\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CW\*(C`mode\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`sum\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`sd\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`uniq\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`var\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`t_test\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`median\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`matrix\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`fisher_test\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CW\*(C`power_t_test\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`var_test\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`dnorm\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`value_counts\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`prcomp\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`pnorm\*(C'\fR.
That is a retype, not a cast: writing
\&\f(CW\*(C`(size_t)items\*(C'\fR at each comparison would silence the warning just as well, but
it would be wrong the day \f(CW\*(C`Stack_off_t\*(C'\fR widens, which is exactly what it exists
to allow. \f(CW\*(C`t_test\*(C'\fR\*(Aqs index was \f(CW\*(C`unsigned short int\*(C'\fR, which drew no warning at
all — integer promotion made the comparison signed — and was the same latent
mistake regardless.
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`Stack_off_t\*(C'\fR arrived in perl 5.39.2 and this distribution supports 5.010, so
the preamble now carries a shim typedef guarded on \f(CW\*(C`PERL_STACK_OFFSET_DEFINED\*(C'\fR,
the macro perl.h defines next to the typedef. On 5.10.1 and 5.12.5 neither the
macro nor the type exists and the shim supplies \f(CW\*(C`I32\*(C'\fR, which is what the stack
offset was on every perl before that.
.PP
The 33 warnings are gone, 25 remain, and no warning category increased —
verified by compiling the before and after trees and diffing the warning sets.
The remaining 25 are unrelated signedness pairs (\f(CW\*(C`size_t\*(C'\fR against \f(CW\*(C`ssize_t\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CW\*(C`IV\*(C'\fR against \f(CW\*(C`size_t\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`STRLEN\*(C'\fR against \f(CW\*(C`ssize_t\*(C'\fR) and are left alone. The full
suite passes on perl 5.10.1 and 5.12.5, the two builds that depend on the shim,
as well as on 5.42.3, 5.44.0 and 5.44.0\-quadmath; and 94 calls covering all 23
retyped functions — positional and named forms, bare lists against arrayrefs,
\&\f(CW\*(C`write_table\*(C'\fR\*(Aqs emitted bytes, and the odd\-argument and unknown\-argument croaks
that this index arithmetic drives — produce identical output before and after.
.PP
\fINV was being computed at double precision on wide builds\fR
.IX Subsection "NV was being computed at double precision on wide builds"
.PP
Every libm call in \f(CW\*(C`LikeR.xs\*(C'\fR was written bare — \f(CWsqrt(x)\fR, \f(CWlog(x)\fR,
\&\f(CWlgamma(x)\fR — and C has no type\-generic \f(CW\*(C`<math.h>\*(C'\fR. Those functions take a
\&\f(CW\*(C`double\*(C'\fR, so on a perl built with \f(CW\*(C`\-Duselongdouble\*(C'\fR or \f(CW\*(C`\-Dusequadmath\*(C'\fR every one
of them converted the \f(CW\*(C`NV\*(C'\fR down to 53 bits of mantissa, computed there, and
converted the result back. Nothing warned and nothing failed to compile; the
answers were simply less accurate than the perl running them. On perl\-5.12.5
(\f(CW\*(C`long double\*(C'\fR), \f(CWsd(1..5)\fR returned exactly the double\-rounded \f(CWsqrt(2.5)\fR,
9.5e\-17 away from the value perl\*(Aqs own \f(CW\*(C`sqrt\*(C'\fR gives.
.PP
All 412 of those calls now go through \f(CW\*(C`nv_*\*(C'\fR macros that paste on the suffix for
the width \f(CW\*(C`NV\*(C'\fR actually is: none for \f(CW\*(C`double\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`l\*(C'\fR for \f(CW\*(C`long double\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`q\*(C'\fR for
\&\f(CW\*(C`_\|_float128\*(C'\fR. The 80 \f(CW\*(C`isnan\*(C'\fR/\f(CW\*(C`isinf\*(C'\fR/\f(CW\*(C`isfinite\*(C'\fR calls became \f(CW\*(C`nv_isnan\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CW\*(C`nv_isinf\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`nv_isfinite\*(C'\fR, which classify by comparing against \f(CW\*(C`NV_MAX\*(C'\fR — the
largest finite \f(CW\*(C`NV\*(C'\fR — rather than calling libm at all. The C99 macros could not
be kept: where a platform does not provide the type\-generic versions,
\&\f(CWisfinite()\fR is a plain \f(CW\*(C`double\*(C'\fR function, and narrowing a large\-but\-finite long
double into it reports the value as infinite rather than merely rounding it.
Perl\*(Aqs own \f(CW\*(C`Perl_isnan\*(C'\fR/\f(CW\*(C`Perl_isinf\*(C'\fR/\f(CW\*(C`Perl_isfinite\*(C'\fR were used up to 0.298 and
could not be kept either: on every perl before 5.22 those route through a
\&\f(CWPerl_fp_class()\fR block in \f(CW\*(C`perl.h\*(C'\fR that has never compiled — the macro is
written with an empty parameter list and compares against \f(CW\*(C`FP_CLASS_*\*(C'\fR names no
\&\f(CW\*(C`<ieeefp.h>\*(C'\fR defines. That block is dead code wherever Configure finds
\&\f(CWisinf()\fR, so it is invisible on Linux and glibc, and live on illumos/Solaris,
where it broke the 0.298 build outright.
.PP
The long\-double row is conditional. The \f(CW\*(C`l\*(C'\fR variants are C99 but some libms —
the thinner BSD ones especially — do not ship the whole set, so \f(CW\*(C`Makefile.PL\*(C'\fR
link\-tests all twenty as a unit and defines \f(CW\*(C`LIKER_HAVE_LONG_DOUBLE_MATH\*(C'\fR only
if every one resolves; otherwise the build falls back to the \f(CW\*(C`double\*(C'\fR functions,
which is exactly what it did before and so cannot regress. \f(CW\*(C`_\|_float128\*(C'\fR needs no
probe: \f(CW\*(C`<quadmath.h>\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`\-lquadmath\*(C'\fR come with the quadmath perl itself, and
the built object was checked with \f(CW\*(C`nm\*(C'\fR — it references \f(CW\*(C`lgammaq\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`expq\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CW\*(C`sqrtq\*(C'\fR and no double\-width libm symbol at all.
.PP
Accuracy on the long\-double build, measured against values that are exact in
binary or known in closed form: \f(CWsd(1..5)\fR is now bit\-identical to perl\*(Aqs
\&\f(CWsqrt(2.5)\fR, and \f(CW\*(C`fisher_test([[3,1],[1,3]])\*(C'\fR moves from 1.5e\-16 to 6.4e\-18
relative error against the exact 17/35. The remaining 6.4e\-18 is an accuracy
floor in that function\*(Aqs own summation, not a width problem — the \f(CW\*(C`_\|_float128\*(C'\fR
build lands on the same figure.
.PP
This costs time where the wide math is software\-emulated: the suite takes 352s
on the quadmath perl, against 67s when it was quietly running on hardware
doubles. The other four perls are unaffected.
.PP
\fIThe build ran itself twice, and clobbered its own Makefile doing it\fR
.IX Subsection "The build ran itself twice, and clobbered its own Makefile doing it"
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`make\*(C'\fR had to be run twice or the \f(CW\*(C`.so\*(C'\fR came out stamped with the wrong version
and refused to load. The cause: ExtUtils::MakeMaker scans the directory for
\&\f(CW\*(C`*.PL\*(C'\fR files to run during the build, and \f(CW\*(C`dev.Makefile.PL\*(C'\fR — a local
convenience wrapper, not part of the distribution — looks like one. It was being
run mid\-build as \f(CW\*(C`perl dev.Makefile.PL dev.Makefile\*(C'\fR, and since it calls
\&\f(CWWriteMakefile()\fR it overwrote the real \f(CW\*(C`Makefile\*(C'\fR with its own: no \f(CW\*(C`DEFINE\*(C'\fR, no
probed C99 flag, and a different \f(CW\*(C`VERSION\*(C'\fR. The second \f(CW\*(C`make\*(C'\fR then rebuilt from
that. \f(CW\*(C`PL_FILES => {}\*(C'\fR turns the scan off; nothing here is generated by a \f(CW\*(C`.PL\*(C'\fR
file.
.PP
The version half was a stale literal: the checked\-in \f(CW\*(C`Makefile.PL\*(C'\fR pinned
\&\f(CW\*(C`VERSION => "0.28"\*(C'\fR while \f(CW\*(C`lib/Stats/LikeR.pm\*(C'\fR had moved to 0.298, and
\&\f(CWXSLoader::load()\fR passes \f(CW$VERSION\fR to a \f(CW\*(C`.so\*(C'\fR compiled with \f(CW\*(C`\-DXS_VERSION\*(C'\fR
from that literal. It now reads \f(CW\*(C`VERSION_FROM => lib/Stats/LikeR.pm\*(C'\fR. One \f(CW\*(C`make\*(C'\fR
after \f(CW\*(C`perl Makefile.PL\*(C'\fR is enough again, and the non\-quadmath builds are about
a third faster for not doing the work twice.
.PP
\fIPortability: Solaris, the BSDs, and vendor compilers\fR
.IX Subsection "Portability: Solaris, the BSDs, and vendor compilers"
.PP
The C99 flag is now probed instead of guessed. \f(CW\*(C`Makefile.PL\*(C'\fR was selecting
\&\f(CW\*(C`\-std=gnu99\*(C'\fR on any compiler whose name matched \f(CW\*(C`/\eb(?:g?cc|clang)\eb/\*(C'\fR, and
\&\f(CW$Config{cc}\fR is plain \f(CW\*(C`cc\*(C'\fR for Oracle Studio on Solaris and for aCC on HP\-UX —
both of which reject that flag outright, so the build failed there before it
compiled a line. Each candidate is now trial\-compiled and the first that works
wins: \f(CW\*(C`\-std=gnu99\*(C'\fR/\f(CW\*(C`\-std=c99\*(C'\fR for gcc and clang, \f(CW\*(C`\-xc99=all\*(C'\fR for Studio,
\&\f(CW\*(C`\-qlanglvl=extc99\*(C'\fR for AIX \f(CW\*(C`xlc\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`\-AC99\*(C'\fR for HP\-UX, and nothing at all for a
compiler already in C99 mode. MSVC is skipped outright, since it warns rather
than errors on switches it does not know and would make the probe settle on a
no\-op.
.PP
Two things that would have failed to compile off Linux are gone. \f(CW\*(C`<strings.h>\*(C'\fR
and its \f(CW\*(C`strcasecmp\*(C'\fR — POSIX\-only, absent on MSVC — are replaced by a small
\&\f(CWstr_ieq_ascii()\fR, which also drops the locale dependency: \f(CWtolower()\fR under a
Turkish locale maps \f(CW\*(C`I\*(C'\fR outside ASCII, which should never decide whether
\&\f(CW"TRUE"\fR matches \f(CW"true"\fR. And bare C99 \f(CW\*(C`restrict\*(C'\fR, used on 151 pointers here,
now has an \f(CW\*(C`#ifdef\*(C'\fR mapping it to \f(CW\*(C`_\|_restrict\*(C'\fR on MSVC and \f(CW\*(C`_\|_restrict_\|_\*(C'\fR on
older gcc, and defining it away where no spelling exists, rather than losing the
annotation.
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`LikeR.xs\*(C'\fR also compiles clean under strict \f(CW\*(C`\-std=c99\*(C'\fR with no GNU extensions,
which is the closest available local proxy for a vendor compiler.
.PP
\fIDead code: \fR\f(BIsample()\fR\fI\*(Aqs private PRNG\fR
.IX Subsection "Dead code: sample()'s private PRNG"
.PP
A splitmix64 generator sat at the top of the file under a comment promising a
PRNG stream separate from \f(CWDrand01()\fR, seeded lazily from \f(CW\*(C`/dev/urandom\*(C'\fR with a
\&\f(CW\*(C`time()^PID\*(C'\fR fallback. None of it was true: no seeding code was ever written, no
caller ever existed, and its state started at a fixed 0, so had anything called
it the "random" sample would have been the same sequence in every process.
\&\f(CWsample()\fR draws from \f(CWDrand01()\fR and always did, which is the behaviour that is
wanted — \f(CWsrand($seed)\fR governs it the way \f(CW\*(C`set.seed()\*(C'\fR governs R. The generator
and its comment are removed.
.PP
\fITests\fR
.IX Subsection "Tests"
.PP
Two files, 273 assertions, and both were checked against a deliberately broken
build rather than merely observed to pass.
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`t/nv_width.t\*(C'\fR fails if the math width ever comes undone. Its sharp assertion
needs no tolerance at all: \f(CWsd(1..5)\fR must be the identical NV to perl\*(Aqs
\&\f(CWsqrt(2.5)\fR, which holds on any width and breaks the moment a \f(CW\*(C`double\*(C'\fR gets in
the way. It is width\-adaptive rather than skipped on a \f(CW\*(C`double\*(C'\fR perl, computing
the NV epsilon of the running build instead of assuming one.
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`t/scale.keywords.t\*(C'\fR covers \f(CWscale()\fR\*(Aqs string options — \f(CW"mean"\fR, \f(CW"sd"\fR,
\&\f(CW"none"\fR, \f(CW"true"\fR, \f(CW"false"\fR, \f(CW""\fR and their case variants — which had no
coverage at all: \f(CW\*(C`t/01.t\*(C'\fR passes only the numeric forms. Expected values come
from R 4.6.1 \f(CWbase::scale()\fR at \f(CWoptions(digits=17)\fR and are frozen in the file,
so it needs no R at run time. Deleting the case fold from \f(CWstr_ieq_ascii()\fR
fails 11 of its assertions; usefully, all 11 are the "off" spellings, because an
unmatched string falls through to \f(CW\*(C`SvTRUE\*(C'\fR and still means "compute it", so
\&\f(CW"MEAN"\fR would keep working while \f(CW"NONE"\fR flipped. That is recorded in the file
so the section is not trusted for more than it proves.
.PP
The suite is 118 files and 18,819 tests, passing on perl 5.10.1, 5.12.5, 5.42.3
(threaded), 5.44.0 and 5.44.0\-quadmath, with no compiler warnings on any of
them.
