\fIassign\fR
.IX Subsection "assign"
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`assign\*(C'\fR now accepts a third kind of column value, \f(CW\*(C`map_cell { ... }\*(C'\fR, for editing an existing column in place — no "copy, substitute, return" boilerplate and no dependence on \f(CW\*(C`s///r\*(C'\fR (unavailable on the older perls this module supports).
.IP \(bu 4
Inside a \f(CW\*(C`map_cell\*(C'\fR block, \f(CW$_\fR is the \fBnamed column\*(Aqs current cell\fR (not the whole row), the block\*(Aqs return value is \fBignored\fR, and the modified \f(CW$_\fR is stored back: \f(CW\*(C`assign($df, \*(AqRes.\*(Aq => map_cell { s/^[A\-Z]:// })\*(C'\fR.
.IP \(bu 4
The row is still available as \f(CW$_[0]\fR (sibling columns), the index as \f(CW$_[1]\fR, and the row key as \f(CW$_[2]\fR (HoH only).
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBUndef/missing cells pass through untouched\fR (undef in → undef out): the block is skipped for them, so \f(CW\*(C`s///\*(C'\fR never warns on an uninitialized value.
.IP \(bu 4
Supported on all three shapes; for HoA the target column must already exist. A plain \f(CW\*(C`sub { ... }\*(C'\fR is unchanged, so \f(CW\*(C`map_cell\*(C'\fR is purely additive.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\f(CW\*(C`map_cell\*(C'\fR is exported alongside \f(CW\*(C`assign\*(C'\fR.
.PP
Tests: \f(CW\*(C`assign.t\*(C'\fR (AoH + HoA) and \f(CW\*(C`assign.HoH.t\*(C'\fR gained \f(CW\*(C`map_cell\*(C'\fR coverage — in\-place \f(CW\*(C`s///\*(C'\fR, \f(CW$_[0]\fR/\f(CW$_[1]\fR/\f(CW$_[2]\fR context, new\-column\-from\-undef, the missing\-HoA\-column death path, and \f(CW\*(C`no_leaks_ok\*(C'\fR guards. Verified building and passing the full suite on perl 5.10.1, 5.12.5 (long\-double), and 5.42.2.
.PP
\fR\f(CI\*(C`group_by\*(C'\fR\fI\fR
.IX Subsection "group_by"
.PP
Fixed group_by to honor all filter hashrefs (option 1)
.PP
Root cause: the XS captured only \fBST\fR\|(3), so every filter hashref after the first was silently dropped — including the README\*(Aqs documented multi\-hashref form.
.PP
Change (LikeR.xs):
\&\- Removed the \fBsingle\-ST\fR\|(3) capture and the filter_hv PREINIT var.
\&\- Added a FOR_EACH_FILTER(body) macro that walks the arg stack from \fBST\fR\|(3) to ST(items\-1), iterating every { column => sub } pair and ANDing them together. It iterates the stack directly rather than heap\-collecting the hashrefs, so a croaking filter sub still can\*(Aqt leak anything (verified). Non\-hashref args are skipped.
\&\- Rewrote the filter loop in all three branches (AoH / HoA / HoH) to use the macro, keeping each branch\*(Aqs own value\-fetch logic.
.PP
One build wrinkle worth noting: xsubpp parses every non\-# line in the inter\-XSUB region as a candidate function signature, so a /* ... */ comment there breaks the build (it tried to parse column => sub / (\fBST\fR\|(3)..) as a signature). I moved the macro\*(Aqs documentation into the XSUB body (real C) and left the macro comment\-free, matching the existing EVAL_FILTER style.
.PP
Tests (t/group_by.HoH.filter.t, 17 assertions):
\&\- HoH single\-column filter, AND filter (both the one\-hashref and separate\-hashref forms now give identical results), no\-match → empty hash, missing/undef target excluded despite passing the filter, and no_leaks_ok
.PP
Mentioning a non\-existent column is now fatal.
